WEBVTT

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:05.520
<v Ira Glass>It's like uncovering a vast secret conspiracy, but one that is right there in front of your eyes.
<v Ira Glass>这就像是揭开了一个巨大的秘密阴谋，但这个阴谋就大喇喇地摆在你的眼前。

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:33.250
<v Ira Glass>5:30 in the morning.  It's still dark outside. And I'm on my way to record some New York City garbage men on their morning route. And the person I'm with, this anthropologist from New York University named Robin Nagle, who's spent years studying how New York City moves its trash. She points to a set of buildings-- these huge, corrugated metal barns-- right on the waterfront at 59th street. This is where the garbage trucks for the island of Manhattan come to dump paper onto barges to be taken away.
<v Ira Glass>清晨五点半。外面天还没亮。我正赶去记录纽约市清洁工们的早班路线。同行的这个人是纽约大学的人类学家，名叫罗宾·内格尔（Robin Nagle），她花了多年时间研究纽约市的垃圾是如何运送的。她指着一组建筑——那些巨大的波纹金属仓库——就在59街的河滨。这里是曼哈顿岛的垃圾车倾倒纸张到驳船上运走的地方。

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:58.290
<v Robin Nagle>I've seen photographs of this as a dumping point-- this exact location-- back dating to the 1890s, when it was wooden planks and horse-drawn carts, dumping directly into scows, where men were waiting with rakes to rake the garbage, which was usually steaming, because it often included ashes and things. And men died by falling off the scows, or getting kicked in the head by the horses, or falling through rotten boards in the planks.
<v Robin Nagle>我见过这里作为垃圾倾倒点的照片——就是这个地方——可以追溯到1890年代，当时这里还是木板路和马车，直接把垃圾倒进平底船里，船上有男人拿着耙子耙开垃圾，垃圾通常还冒着热气，因为里面经常夹杂着灰烬之类的东西。当时有人从平底船上掉下去摔死，或者被马踢到头部，或者从腐烂的木板缝隙中跌落丧命。

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:08.130
<v Ira Glass>There are no signs-- nothing to indicate that this is part of city government-- except for one city seal that is actually too faded to read. Which is not unusual.
<v Ira Glass>这里没有任何标志——没有任何迹象表明这里属于市政府——只有一个已经褪色到几乎认不出来的城市徽章。这并不罕见。

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:25.220
<v Robin Nagle>You'll see a lot of sanitation facilities-- there's no name over the door, there's nothing telling you it's a sanitation facility. The perception of sanitation in the public's eye is generally not great, and so it's easier if they can go below radar.
<v Robin Nagle>你会看到很多环卫设施——门上没有名字，没有任何标示告诉你这是一个环卫设施。公众对环卫部门的印象通常不是很好，所以如果他们能保持低调，事情就会好办得多。

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:35.580
<v Ira Glass>On maps of the city, you can see these piers, but it's not labeled what they are. Just like Fresh Kills on Staten Island, the largest landfill on the planet, for years has appeared on maps as a public park.
<v Ira Glass>在城市地图上，你能看到这些码头，但上面没有标注它们是什么。就像斯塔滕岛上的弗莱士基尔斯（Fresh Kills）一样，作为地球上最大的垃圾填埋场，多年来在地图上一直被标注为一个市民公园。

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:42.820
<v Ira Glass>People want everything having to do with garbage to be invisible. We want it out of sight. We don't want to think about it, don't want to be associated with it.
<v Ira Glass>人们希望一切与垃圾有关的东西都是隐形的。我们希望它们眼不见心不烦。我们不想去想它，不想和它扯上关系。

00:01:42.820 --> 00:02:00.610
<v Ira Glass>One guy who I interviewed for today's radio program has a kind of high up job in the department of sanitation. Makes tons of money, lots of responsibility. And he got in contact after the interview to ask that I not use his name. His neighbors didn't know that he worked for the sanitation department, and he didn't want them to know.
<v Ira Glass>我为今天的广播节目采访过的一个人，在环卫部门担任高层职务。赚大钱，责任重大。采访结束后他联系了我，要求我不要用他的名字。他的邻居们不知道他在环卫部门工作，他也不想让他们知道。

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:12.260
<v Ira Glass>One sanitation worker told Robin that one day he was picking up a bag of trash and a lady's walking a poodle. And the lady lets the poodle start peeing on the bag as he's grabbing it. Like he's not even there.
<v Ira Glass>有个清洁工告诉罗宾，有一天他正在捡一袋垃圾，一个女人正遛着一只贵宾犬。当他去抓垃圾袋时，那女人竟然让贵宾犬在垃圾袋上撒尿。就好像他根本不存在一样。

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:33.470
<v Robin Nagle>Sanitation workers will tell you that they're invisible. How can a pedestrian let her dog pee on a bag where a man is reaching for that bag and has his face not 18 inches from the dog who then lefts his leg to take a leak? That man must be invisible to that woman, that she would let that happen without giving it a second thought.
<v Robin Nagle>清洁工会告诉你，他们是隐形的。一个行人怎么能让她的狗在一袋垃圾上撒尿，而此时一个男人正伸手去拿那袋垃圾，他的脸距离那只随后抬起腿撒尿的狗还不到18英寸？那个男人在那个女人眼里一定是个隐形人，否则她怎么会任由这种事情发生而连想都不想一下。

00:02:33.470 --> 00:03:11.600
<v Ira Glass>Today on our radio show, we take what's invisible and we make it visible. Stories of garbage, yours and mine. We live in a way that produces more waste than any people have in the history of civilization. We come here today not to feel bad about that, not to condemn that-- I know this is a public radio show, but I make a guarantee to you right now that this is not a radio program today about feeling bad about the immense volume of trash poking around the edges of all of our lives. No, no, no. This is a radio program about understanding the actual fact of it, finally.
<v Ira Glass>今天在我们的广播节目中，我们要让隐形的东西变得可见。关于垃圾的故事，你的，还有我的。我们的生活方式所产生的废弃物，比文明史上任何时期的人类都要多。我们今天来这里不是为了对此感到难过，也不是为了谴责——我知道这是一个公共广播节目，但我现在向你保证，今天的广播节目绝对不是为了让我们为那些潜伏在我们每个人生活边缘的巨量垃圾感到内疚。不，不，不。这期广播节目是为了最终理解它的事实本身。

00:03:11.600 --> 00:04:00.450
<v Ira Glass>Our program today in three acts. Act One. Oh, Mr. San Man. In which we get our hands on your trash, yours, with the men who do it every day. Act Two. Except For the Smell, I Think I Have a Crush On You. There are squatters who build entire neighborhoods on top of rotting garbage heaps in Mexico. They scavenge in the trash piles for their living. We go there for a little love story. Act Three. I am a Legitimate Businessman... Waste Management. In that act, the secret recordings that ended mob control of New York garbage. We got them. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>我们今天的节目分为三幕。第一幕，“哦，环卫侠”（Oh, Mr. San Man）。我们将和每天处理垃圾的人一起，亲手接触你抛弃的垃圾。第二幕，“除了气味，我想我爱上你了”（Except For the Smell, I Think I Have a Crush On You）。在墨西哥，有些擅自占地者在腐烂的垃圾堆上建立起了整个社区。他们在垃圾堆里拾荒维生。我们去那里寻找一段小小的爱情故事。第三幕，“我是个合法的商人……废弃物管理公司”（I am a Legitimate Businessman... Waste Management）。在那一幕里，我们有终结黑帮控制纽约垃圾产业的秘密录音。我们拿到了。请继续收听。

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:03.670
<v Ira Glass>Act One. Oh, Mr. San Man.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕。哦，环卫侠。

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:27.360
<v Ira Glass>The government has a number where they take the amount of waste-- residential, commercial, industrial waste-- created in this country, and they divide it by how many of us there are to tell us how much garbage is produced per person by all of us just living our normal lives in this country. That number? 4.8 pounds per person per day.
<v Ira Glass>政府有一个数据，统计了这个国家产生的废物总量——包括居民垃圾、商业垃圾、工业垃圾，然后除以我们的人口总数，以此告诉我们，在这个国家里，单靠我们每个人过着正常生活，人均会产生多少垃圾。那个数字是多少？每人每天4.8磅。

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:31.820
<v Ira Glass>One rainy morning I wanted to see exactly what that means on the ground.
<v Ira Glass>一个下雨的早晨，我想亲眼看看这在现实中意味着什么。

00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:33.240
<v Robin Nagle>We're doing this block, right?
<v Robin Nagle>我们要做这条街，对吧？

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:35.600
<v Andre Ramos>No, the next block.
<v Andre Ramos>不，下一条街。

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:36.820
<v Robin Nagle>I'll get on the running board.
<v Robin Nagle>我上去踩踏板。

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:39.310
<v Andre Ramos>OK. You sit there. I get on the other side.
<v Andre Ramos>好的。你坐那儿。我从另一边上。

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:51.830
<v Ira Glass>For more than a year, Robin Nagle-- who you just heard from-- has been riding around with New York City sanitation workers, doing the job alongside them, as part of her research. She wears the green uniform. She slings trash.
<v Ira Glass>一年多来，你刚才听到的罗宾·内格尔一直跟着纽约市的清洁工到处跑，作为她研究的一部分，她和他们一起干活。她穿着绿色制服。她搬运垃圾。

00:04:51.830 --> 00:05:05.910
<v Ira Glass>I went out with with her and two guys. Andre Ramos-- who reminds me a little bit of a Latino Billy Crystal-- in his early 40s, goatee, turning a little gray, chatty and outgoing. And his partner, an older, rather dignified man.
<v Ira Glass>我和她以及另外两个人一起出车。安德雷·拉莫斯（Andre Ramos）——让我想起一点拉丁裔比利·克里斯托（Billy Crystal）的感觉——40多岁，留着山羊胡，胡子有点发灰，健谈且外向。还有他的搭档，一个年长、颇具尊严的男士。

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:17.560
<v Roger Patton>My name is Roger Patton. But because my name is Patton, everyone on the job calls me General. But I'm not a general. I don't behave as one.
<v Roger Patton>我叫罗杰·巴顿（Roger Patton）。但因为我姓巴顿，工作中的每个人都叫我将军。但我不是将军。我行事并不像个将军。

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:28.210
<v Ira Glass>The General drives. Ramos rides the running board. And when they tell me the amount of trash that the two of them lift and throw into the back of the truck on their morning route, Ramos gives me the number in tons.
<v Ira Glass>将军负责开车。拉莫斯站在脚踏板上。当他们告诉我，在早晨的线路上，他俩要搬运并扔进卡车后部的垃圾总量时，拉莫斯给出了一个以吨为单位的数字。

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:32.340
<v Andre Ramos>14.4 is our target weight, or better. 14.4.
<v Andre Ramos>14.4吨是我们的目标重量，或者更多。14.4吨。

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:36.990
<v Ira Glass>So that means that you're personally picking up seven tons of garbage a day?
<v Ira Glass>那也就是说，你一个人一天要捡七吨垃圾？

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:40.040
<v Andre Ramos>More or less, yes.
<v Andre Ramos>差不多吧，是的。

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:41.300
<v Ira Glass>That seems crazy.
<v Ira Glass>这听起来太疯狂了。

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:43.850
<v Andre Ramos>Really? Why?
<v Andre Ramos>真的吗？为什么？

00:05:43.850 --> 00:05:47.540
<v Ira Glass>Because it just doesn't seem like a person can even pick up--
<v Ira Glass>因为这听上去根本就不像是一个人能——

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:55.600
<v Andre Ramos>After a while, all you see is black bags, and you just keep throwing it in there, throwing it in there. Until you fill this truck up.
<v Andre Ramos>过了一会儿，你眼里就只有黑色的垃圾袋，然后你就一直往里扔，一直往里扔。直到把这辆卡车装满。

00:05:55.600 --> 00:06:06.940
<v Ira Glass>The sheer athleticism that this requires is something that most of us don't think about, usually. When Ramos talks about what it was like to first do this job, throw trash all day for a living, he sighs.
<v Ira Glass>这需要纯粹的体能，而这通常是我们大多数人不会去想的。当拉莫斯谈到他刚开始做这份工作、每天靠扔垃圾谋生时的感受时，他叹了口气。

00:06:06.940 --> 00:06:18.290
<v Andre Ramos>It was hard. It really hard. Because you're not used to the lifting, the dragging, the pulling. Everything is different.
<v Andre Ramos>很难。真的很难。因为你不习惯那种举重、拖拽、拉扯。一切都变了。

00:06:18.290 --> 00:06:23.940
<v Roger Patton>Because on this job, everyone hurts. Your back, your shoulders, your legs.
<v Roger Patton>因为在这份工作里，每个人都会受伤。你的背部、肩膀、腿部。

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:29.400
<v Andre Ramos>My body, we saw plenty of Tiger Balm, plenty of Ben-Gay.
<v Andre Ramos>我的身体，我们用了大量的虎标万金油，大量的本杰（Ben-Gay）止痛膏。

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:34.340
<v Ira Glass>So how many months did it take before you were able to lift comfortably, and at the end of the day, you weren't wiped out?
<v Ira Glass>那你花了多少个月，才能够轻松地举起垃圾，并且在一天结束时不会累垮？

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:48.460
<v Andre Ramos>I tell you, it took more than a few months. It took the years. It really did. Yes. Because this is something, you know, you've got to tailor your body into. It's not something that you get used to overnight, no matter if you were a hard worker all your life.
<v Andre Ramos>我告诉你，花了不止几个月。花了几年时间。真的花了几年。是的。因为这需要你用身体去适应。这不是你一朝一夕就能习惯的事情，不管你一辈子是不是个刻苦耐劳的人。

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:54.170
<v Ira Glass>As with a lot of physically demanding jobs, it's all about pacing yourself, conserving your energy where you can.
<v Ira Glass>就像许多对体力要求极高的工作一样，关键在于掌握好节奏，在能省力的地方保存体力。

00:06:54.170 --> 00:07:03.410
<v Ira Glass>A typical stop. The General pulls up the truck to 30 trash bags set out in front of an apartment building on the Upper West Side. He tries to get the truck close to the bags.
<v Ira Glass>一个典型的停靠点。将军把卡车停在西区一栋公寓楼前摆放的30个垃圾袋旁。他尽量把卡车停得离垃圾袋近一些。

00:07:03.410 --> 00:07:16.410
<v Ira Glass>Most of the bags are at 60, maybe 70 pounds. Small enough for Ramos or the General or Robin to carry alone. Some buildings use these big, black sausage bags packed by compactors. They do those together.
<v Ira Glass>大部分袋子重60或70磅。足够拉莫斯、将军或罗宾一个人搬运。有些建筑使用由压缩机打包的大型黑色香肠状垃圾袋。那些他们得一起搬。

00:07:16.410 --> 00:07:20.810
<v Andre Ramos>This one is heavy. What would you say, like what?
<v Andre Ramos>这个很重。你觉得有多重？

00:07:20.810 --> 00:07:24.870
<v Roger Patton>It's about 120 pounds. That's about 120 pounds.
<v Roger Patton>大概120磅。差不多120磅。

00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:33.270
<v Andre Ramos>This bag is heavy. In this case, we try to get as close to the truck as possible, and we both lift at the same time.
<v Andre Ramos>这个袋子很重。这种情况下，我们尽量靠近卡车，然后两人同时抬。

00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:38.720
<v Ira Glass>The General, by the way, is doing this-- lifting seven tons a day-- at 58 years old.
<v Ira Glass>顺便说一下，将军今年58岁了，他依然在做这种工作——每天举起七吨垃圾。

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:40.060
<v Andre Ramos>Come around on this side.
<v Andre Ramos>到这边来。

00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:41.720
<v Roger Patton>Come over here.
<v Roger Patton>过来这里。

00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:45.440
<v Ira Glass>They wave me away from the back of the truck to the side.
<v Ira Glass>他们把我从卡车后部挥手赶到侧面。

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:54.400
<v Roger Patton>Never stand behind the hopper while it's running. It will come spewing out on you. So it's best to stand to the side.
<v Roger Patton>运转时千万不要站在料斗后面。垃圾会喷溅到你身上。所以最好站在侧面。

00:07:54.400 --> 00:08:17.340
<v Andre Ramos>And the reason for that is that, odds this is compressed. It can go to 3,000 pounds per square inch. And it's crushing these bags, 3,000 pounds per square inch. An object can come flying out of there. It's like somebody is literally shooting a bullet at you. It could be a nail, glass or something. It can do damage.
<v Andre Ramos>原因在于，这些东西被压缩了。压力可以达到每平方英寸3,000磅。它正在压碎这些袋子，压力高达每平方英寸3,000磅。会有物体从里面飞出来。就像有人真的在向你开枪一样。可能是钉子、玻璃或其他东西。这会造成伤害。

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:27.800
<v Ira Glass>There are all sorts of ways to get hurt on this job. Guys ding their legs into car bumpers. They get scraped or cut by glass or metal that slices through the trash bags. Guys get hit by cars.
<v Ira Glass>在这份工作中有各种各样的受伤方式。小伙子们的腿会撞到汽车保险杠上。他们会被划破垃圾袋的玻璃或金属刮伤或割伤。小伙子们会被车撞。

00:08:27.800 --> 00:09:06.160
<v Ira Glass>At one stop, Robin and the General dragged over this rust-colored couch and they placed it in the hopper. It's so big that half of it sticks out the end of the truck. Ramos makes the blade of the compactor come down, and-- and I have to say this-- but one moment, there's a couch there, and the next moment, it is completely flat. Snapped from three dimensions to two. Then the blade cycles around slowly crushes this big, rust-colored rectangle in with the kitchen garbage and the other trash-- and it's gone.
<v Ira Glass>在一个停靠点，罗宾和将军拖过来一个铁锈色的沙发，把它放进了料斗里。它太大了，以至于有一半露在卡车外面。拉莫斯让压缩机的刀片降下来，然后——我不得不这么说——上一秒那里还有一个沙发，下一秒它就彻底变平了。从三维瞬间压缩成二维。接着刀片循环转动，把这个巨大的铁锈色长方体和厨房垃圾以及其他垃圾一起缓缓压碎——然后它就消失了。

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:12.820
<v Ira Glass>In just a couple of hours we smash a TV, and a couple of futon frames, and a bookcase.
<v Ira Glass>在短短几个小时内，我们砸碎了一台电视机、几个榻榻米床架和一个书柜。

00:09:12.820 --> 00:09:19.910
<v Andre Ramos>I've seen refrigerators crushed to nothing. It's able to crush almost anything. Almost anything.
<v Andre Ramos>我见过冰箱被压得粉碎。它几乎能压碎一切。几乎一切。

00:09:19.910 --> 00:09:43.290
<v Ira Glass>Given how it's just one of the unquestioned facts of modern life that somebody's going to come and pick up almost anything, almost anything today, it's amazing to think just how recent an innovation garbage collection is. Cities have been around for thousands of years. And for most of that time, spoiled food and household waste was just left outside, on the street, just to rot. And Robin, who studies this, says that's what New York was like.
<v Ira Glass>考虑到现代生活中有这样一个不容置疑的事实：不管是什么东西，今天总会有人来收走几乎任何东西、几乎所有的东西，想想垃圾收集这项创新有多么年轻，真是令人惊奇。城市已经存在了数千年。而在那大部分时间里，变质的食物和生活垃圾就被直接丢在外面、街上，任其腐烂。研究这一课题的罗宾说，过去的纽约就是这个样子。

00:09:43.290 --> 00:09:58.470
<v Robin Nagle>The city was, compared to today, unimaginably filthy. Gunk and mud in the streets, ankle-deep with rotting horse carcasses and piles of animal dung. Just really unimaginably dirty.
<v Robin Nagle>跟今天相比，这座城市脏得难以想象。街道上满是污垢和泥泞，脚踝深的腐烂马尸和堆积如山的动物粪便。真的脏得难以想象。

00:09:58.470 --> 00:10:11.710
<v Ira Glass>This finally changed in the late 1800s. New York created a sanitation department first in 1881, but it didn't actually accomplish anything until 1896, when a civil engineer slash Civil War vet took over.
<v Ira Glass>这种情况终于在19世纪末发生了改变。纽约在1881年首次成立了环卫部门，但直到1896年才真正有所作为，当时一位土木工程师兼内战老兵接管了该部门。

00:10:11.710 --> 00:10:39.330
<v Robin Nagle>In 1896, a guy was appointed commissioner named Colonel George Wearing. And he came in and he turned it around. He had some very savvy ideas. He put the men in white uniforms to suggest cleanliness, and he gave them pith helmets, like the local cops of the day wore, to suggest power-- enforcement power. And he set out the routes. He gave them standards they had to meet each day. He organized, he bureaucratized the job, but he also made the men accountable.
<v Robin Nagle>1896年，任命了一位名叫乔治·威林（George Wearing）上校的专员。他上任后扭转了局面。他有一些非常聪明的点子。他让清洁工穿上白制服以暗示清洁，并给他们戴上类似当时当地警察所戴的太阳帽，以暗示权力——执法权力。他还规划了路线。他给他们制定了每天必须达到的标准。他进行了组织规划，使工作官僚化，同时也让这些人承担起责任。

00:10:39.330 --> 00:10:55.940
<v Robin Nagle>So there are before and after pictures of streets around the city that were this ankle-deep muck before he came. And then after he came, they're pristine. You can see the curbs very nicely defined. There's no garbage anywhere. And they got the nickname White Wings because of their uniform.
<v Robin Nagle>所以有他上任前和上任后的城市街道对比照片，在他来之前，这里是没脚深的污泥。而在他上任之后，街道一尘不染。你可以看到非常清晰的路边石。到处都没有垃圾。因为他们的制服，他们得到了“白翼”（White Wings）的绰号。

00:10:55.940 --> 00:11:15.970
<v Robin Nagle>And the men had an interesting status now that they have since lost. There were parades down Fifth Avenue every year for a long time. And the White Wings were heralded as the heroes who had cleaned the streets effectively for the first time in the city's history.
<v Robin Nagle>这些人现在拥有了一种他们后来失去了的有趣地位。在很长一段时间里，第五大道上每年都会举行游行。“白翼”被欢呼为英雄，他们在城市历史上第一次有效地清理了街道。

00:11:15.970 --> 00:11:23.710
<v Roger Patton>See how this is put out, with the nails standing up? They're supposed to drive the nails down or take them out.
<v Roger Patton>看这个是怎么摆出来的，钉子朝上？他们应该把钉子砸进去或者拔掉。

00:11:23.710 --> 00:11:42.000
<v Ira Glass>On 103rd street, the General shows me these boards left with the trash-- maybe eight feet long, maybe ten feet long-- with nails sticking out, like a medieval weapon. From the same stop, Ramos shows me a piece of metal eight inches long sticking out of a bag, ready to slice into your leg if you don't spot it. It's like Russian roulette, he says.
<v Ira Glass>在103街，将军向我展示了这些随垃圾丢弃的木板——大约八英尺长，或者十英尺长——上面扎着钉子，就像中世纪的武器一样。在同一个停靠点，拉莫斯向我展示了一块从袋子里伸出来的八英寸长的金属片，如果你没看见，它随时准备割进你的腿里。他说，这就像俄罗斯轮盘赌。

00:11:42.000 --> 00:12:03.520
<v Andre Ramos>They throw everything in here. They throw all kinds of-- this is garbage, after all. They don't think that another human being has to come and pick this up, that has responsibility, that has loved ones, just here for eight hours, just wants to do his job out here, safe as possible, and make it through the day. People don't consider the fact there's another human being's got to pick up this. So don't just throw it in there. After all, it's just garbage.
<v Andre Ramos>他们什么都往这里扔。他们扔各种各样的东西——这毕竟是垃圾。他们不会去想，另一个活生生的人必须来清理这些，这个人有责任，有挚爱的人，他只是在这里工作八个小时，只想在这里尽可能安全地做好他的工作，度过这一天。人们根本不考虑这样一个事实：必须由另一个活生生的人来捡起这些东西。所以不要随便把它扔进去。毕竟，这只是垃圾。

00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:32.950
<v Andre Ramos>So yeah. One time this truck, about a year and a half ago, we went to a Jersey dump and came up radioactive. Yeah. They took us off the scale, and they had one of the personnel come with, I believe it's a Geiger counter? And they went around the truck and took a reading. And it came up radioactive. We had radioactive material in there. Where we picked it up, we got no idea. Only we know it was in one of the black bags.
<v Andre Ramos>所以，是的。有一次，大约一年半前，这辆卡车去了新泽西的一个垃圾场，结果测出了放射性。是的。他们让我们离开地磅，其中一个工作人员带着，我相信那是盖革计数器吧？他们围着卡车转了一圈并读了数。结果显示有放射性。我们里面有放射性物质。我们在哪里捡到的，完全不知道。我们只知道它藏在其中一个黑袋子里。

00:12:32.950 --> 00:12:54.400
<v Ira Glass>One of the most famous stories among New York City san men is the story of Michael Hanley, a san man on the last stop of the day in November 1996 who was standing behind the truck when a jog of hydrofluoric acid in the trash exploded, shooting out of the hopper and killing him.
<v Ira Glass>在纽约市的清洁工中，最著名的故事之一是迈克尔·汉利（Michael Hanley）的故事。1996年11月，在当天的最后一站，身为清洁工的他在卡车后面站着时，垃圾里的一罐氢氟酸突然爆炸，从料斗里喷涌而出，将他当场炸死。

00:12:54.400 --> 00:13:34.280
<v Ira Glass>It's a coveted job to be a New York City san man. When they last gave the qualifying test, 30,000 people took it. The General waited five years after passing the exam before a job came open, which is typical. And though the work is grueling, the pay-- if you're actually on a truck-- starts at $40,000 and can go to $60 after just five years. A good winter, meaning one with lots of overtime for clearing snow-- they clear snow, too-- can make for a $90,000 year for a senior guy.
<v Ira Glass>在纽约市当一名清洁工是一份令人艳羡的工作。他们上次举行资格考试时，有3万人参加。将军通过考试后等了五年才等到一个空缺岗位，这很典型。尽管工作极其辛苦，但如果你真的上了卡车工作，起薪是4万美元，仅仅五年后就能涨到6万美元。如果遇到一个好冬天——也就是有很多清雪加班费的冬天（他们也负责清雪）——一个资深老员工一年可以挣到9万美元。

00:13:34.280 --> 00:13:47.230
<v Ira Glass>Today everything goes smoothly. The trash is usually put out neatly for us, in bags, because we're in pretty good neighborhoods. We block traffic for ten minutes at a time. We get rained on until we're soaked. The rain actually never lets up all morning.
<v Ira Glass>今天一切都很顺利。垃圾通常被整齐地放在袋子里给我们收走，因为我们所在的街区相当不错。我们每次挡住交通十分钟。我们被雨淋得透湿。事实上，整个早晨雨一直没停过。

00:13:47.230 --> 00:14:18.020
<v Ira Glass>At some point, I finish a take out coffee that I'd been drinking, and I start looking around the street for a garbage can to throw the cup in. And then I remember-- I have a garbage truck right here. I walk around to the back and I toss the cup right into the hopper. It feels good, tossing it straight into the truck. Like the feeling that you get driving to a farm, picking apples off the trees yourself.
<v Ira Glass>某个时刻，我喝完了一杯外卖咖啡，开始在街上找垃圾桶想把杯子扔进去。然后我想起来——我这里就有一辆垃圾车。我走到后面，直接把杯子扔进了料斗里。直接把它扔进卡车里感觉真好。就像开车去农场、自己从树上摘苹果时的那种感觉。

00:14:18.020 --> 00:14:32.490
<v Ira Glass>Mostly the thing that hits you as you ride around on a garbage truck is just how much stuff we throw out each day. One bag after another, block after block, all smashed together in the hopper, where everything merges into a disgusting, liquidy sludge.
<v Ira Glass>坐在垃圾车上，最深切的感受莫过于我们每天扔掉的东西实在是太多了。一个接一个的袋子，一条接一条的街道，全都在料斗里被砸碎，所有东西融合成恶心的液体污泥。

00:14:32.490 --> 00:14:42.430
<v Ira Glass>Robin teaches a class on the anthropology of garbage. She's writing a book about garbage men. So needless to say, she has thought about this quite a lot-- about the sheer volume of waste that we create today.
<v Ira Glass>罗宾教一门关于垃圾人类学的课程。她正在写一本关于清洁工的书。不用说，她对此思考得相当多——关于我们今天产生的庞大废物量。

00:14:42.430 --> 00:14:52.900
<v Robin Nagle>Trash today-- the meaning of trash today, in part, is about a different kind of relationship to time that we have now. We depend on disposability, to move at a certain kind of speed.
<v Robin Nagle>今天的垃圾——今天垃圾的意义，部分在于我们现在对时间有一种不同的关系。我们依赖用完即弃的消费模式，以某种特定的速度生活。

00:14:52.900 --> 00:15:07.390
<v Robin Nagle>You and I had a cup of coffee this morning. It wouldn't occur to us to save the cup, rinse it out, use it again. It wasn't a ceramic cup. If it were a ceramic cup, I've got to keep it somewhere it's not going to get knocked around in my backpack and broken. I've got to bring it home and wash it, carry it out again. I have to remember it the next day.
<v Robin Nagle>你和我今天早上都喝了一杯咖啡。我们不会想到把杯子留下来、冲洗干净、再次使用。它不是陶瓷杯。如果它是个陶瓷杯，我就得把它放在背包里不会被撞坏和打破的地方。我必须把它带回家洗干净，然后再带出来。我第二天还得记得带上它。

00:15:07.390 --> 00:15:19.510
<v Robin Nagle>There's even a whole way in which our mental life is organized that depends on disposability. I don't have to pay attention to-- when I go to the grocery store, once in a while, I remember to take a cloth sack, but usually I don't, because they'll give me a plastic one. I know that.
<v Robin Nagle>甚至我们的心理生活组织方式也完全依赖于用完即弃的消费模式。我不需要注意——当我偶尔去杂货店时，我会记得带一个布袋子，但通常我不会带，因为他们会给我一个塑料袋。我知道这一点。

00:15:19.510 --> 00:15:22.670
<v Ira Glass>I know, but it's nice to not have to think about those things.
<v Ira Glass>我知道，但不用去想那些事情感觉很好。

00:15:22.670 --> 00:15:44.180
<v Robin Nagle>Of course, of course. One of the things I used to do was save things to use again. And I've stopped doing that. Because it is much easier and much more-- I don't know. I'm not saying-- I mean, I would be the first person to have a very, very hard time slowing down-- I don't know, it's just simpler. The way we live now.
<v Robin Nagle>当然，当然。我以前常做的事情之一就是把东西留着以后再用。我已经不这么做了。因为那样要容易得多，而且更——我不知道。我不是说——我的意思是，如果要放慢生活节奏，我绝对会是最难做到这一点的人——我不知道，这样更简单。我们现在的生存方式。

00:15:44.180 --> 00:16:13.490
<v Robin Nagle>But again, this is how we organize this socially. That's new. We didn't live like that before. It's new with industrialization. It's new with plastics technologies and plastic sciences that make light-- I mean, it's now cheaper to buy a new VCR than to fix a VCR, to buy a new pair of shoes than to get them repaired, to buy it rather than repair it.
<v Robin Nagle>但话又说回来，这就是我们在社会上组织生活的方式。这是新鲜事物。我们以前不这样生活。这是工业化带来的新现象。这是塑料技术和塑料科学带来的新现象，它们让生活变轻——我的意思是，现在买一台新的录像机比修理一台录像机更便宜，买一双新鞋比拿去修理更便宜，买新的总比修理合算。

00:16:13.490 --> 00:16:18.780
<v Ira Glass>After two hours on the street-- it's 8 AM-- time for the union-mandated 15 minute break.
<v Ira Glass>在街上干了两个小时后——现在是早上8点——到了工会规定的15分钟休息时间。

00:16:18.780 --> 00:16:36.940
<v Roger Patton>I call a 15 minutes of fame. You can eat what you want in 15 minutes. You can relieve yourself for 15 minutes. You could sign autographs for 15 minutes. It's our break and the union fought hard for it, and why shouldn't we take it?
<v Roger Patton>我称之为成名15分钟。你可以在15分钟内吃你想吃的东西。你可以在15分钟内解决个人问题。你可以在15分钟内签名。这是我们的休息时间，工会为此做了坚决的斗争，我们为什么不休息呢？

00:16:36.940 --> 00:16:49.940
<v Ira Glass>The General parks the truck and we duck into a diner on Broadway. Ramos gets a vitamin water. Somebody gets a bagel. There are coffees. And I ask the guys if they ever get overwhelmed or disgusted by all the trash. They mostly shrug this off.
<v Ira Glass>将军把卡车停好，我们钻进百老汇的一家小餐馆。拉莫斯点了一瓶维生素水。有人点了一个百吉饼。还有咖啡。我问这几个小伙子，他们有没有对所有的垃圾感到不堪重负或恶心。他们大多耸耸肩，不以为然。

00:16:49.940 --> 00:16:56.390
<v Roger Patton>Yes, we do. But we know it's a job, so therefore, somebody has to do it. And it's part of our job.
<v Roger Patton>是的，我们会。但我们知道这是一份工作，所以总得有人来做。这是我们工作的一部分。

00:16:56.390 --> 00:17:03.720
<v Andre Ramos>It's part of the job. And you don't think too much of it. You just see it as what it is-- waste.
<v Andre Ramos>这是工作的一部分。你不会想太多。你只会把它看作它本来面目——废物。

00:17:03.720 --> 00:17:09.830
<v Ira Glass>I know. But just riding around with you, just this little bit, I just feel like, God, people have a lot of crap.
<v Ira Glass>我知道。但就跟你们一起兜风这么一会儿，我就觉得，天哪，人们怎么有这么多垃圾。

00:17:09.830 --> 00:17:20.560
<v Roger Patton>We do try to treat it with the utmost respect and-- [LAUGHTER]
<v Roger Patton>我们确实试图以最大的尊重来对待它，而且——[笑声]

00:17:20.560 --> 00:17:41.370
<v Ira Glass>15 minutes goes fast and we stand up to leave. After a morning of picking up other people's trash, Ramos leaves his empty vitamin water bottle at the table. He doesn't say anything about it. He doesn't look back. Throwing that out is somebody else's job.
<v Ira Glass>15分钟过得很快，我们起身准备离开。经过一个早晨清理别人的垃圾后，拉莫斯把他的空维生素水瓶留在了桌上。他什么也没说。他没有回头。把那东西扔掉是别人的工作。

00:17:41.370 --> 00:18:23.440
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "MR SANDMAN" BY GOB]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 戈布乐队的《沙人先生》]

00:18:23.440 --> 00:19:43.600
<v Ira Glass>Coming up. Taking your teenage kid to the garbage heap where you spent your youth. Literally, the garbage heap. In Tijuana, how one American teenager reacts to his dad's trash-filled past. This in a minute from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来。带你正处于青春期的孩子去你度过青少年的垃圾堆。字面意义上的垃圾堆。在蒂华纳，一个美国少年如何看待他父亲充满垃圾的过去。稍后我们将从芝加哥公共广播电台和国际公共广播电台继续我们的节目。

00:19:43.600 --> 00:20:02.890
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose some theme, bring you a variety of different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's program, Garbage. We make the world of garbage, a world that's usually invisible to us, visible. Here on the radio where, I guess, everything is invisible. We've arrived at act two of our program.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《此生美国》（This American Life）。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。每周在我们的节目中，我们当然都会选择一个主题，为您带来围绕该主题的各种不同故事。今天的节目是：垃圾。我们让垃圾的世界——一个对我们而言通常隐形的世界——变得可见。在这里，在广播里，我想，一切都是隐形的。我们来到了节目的第二幕。

00:20:02.890 --> 00:20:07.280
<v Ira Glass>Act Two. Except For the Smell, I Think I Have a Crush On You.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕。除了气味，我想我爱上你了。

00:20:07.280 --> 00:20:23.870
<v Ira Glass>Luis Alberto Urrea was born in Tijuana and grew up moving back and forth between Tijuana and San Diego. When he got older, he moved away from the border and all its poverty. Now he lives in the Chicago suburbs. And recently, he headed back to Tijuana, to the border, for the first time in years.
<v Ira Glass>路易斯·阿尔贝托·乌雷亚（Luis Alberto Urrea）出生于蒂华纳，在蒂华纳和圣迭戈之间不断搬迁的长大。长大后，他搬离了边境及其所有的贫困。现在他住在芝加哥郊区。最近，他多年来首次重返蒂华纳，重返边境。

00:20:23.870 --> 00:20:50.080
<v Luis Urrea>I used to work with the garbage pickers of Tijuana's municipal garbage dump. People who survived by scavenging through the trash. They'd recycle glass and aluminum for cash, find food and clothes they kept for themselves, all just minutes from downtown San Diego, which was not far from the border fence. I was translator for an American missionary group that brought them food, clothing, medical attention.
<v Luis Urrea>我过去曾在蒂华纳的城市垃圾填埋场的垃圾捡拾工中工作。那些靠在垃圾堆里拾荒维生的人。他们把玻璃和铝回收换取现金，寻找自己留下的食物和衣服，这一切都距离圣迭戈市中心只有几分钟路程，离边境铁丝网也不远。我曾是一个美国传教士团体的翻译，该团体为他们带来食物、衣服和医疗护理。

00:20:50.080 --> 00:21:02.920
<v Luis Urrea>In the years since I'd left, many things had changed. The trash pickers had taken over the dump, squatting on the land and building homes atop the garbage. And I had gotten married and started a family.
<v Luis Urrea>在我离开的这些年里，许多事情都变了。拾荒者接管了垃圾填埋场，擅自占领土地，并在垃圾堆上建造了房屋。而我也已经结婚并组建了家庭。

00:21:02.920 --> 00:21:37.490
<v Luis Urrea>My wife and I decided to return to the dump so my old friends living there could meet my new family. In particular, we were going to find Negra. When I first met Negra, she was seven. She was a skinny little dark girl with bare feet in a sack dress, one pair of panties. That was all she had. Her family lived in a one-room shack with two beds inside, a little wood-burning stove. We kept in touch for decades. I'm godfather to one of her kids.
<v Luis Urrea>我和妻子决定返回垃圾填埋场，好让住在那里老朋友们见见我的新家人。特别是，我们要去找内格拉（Negra）。我刚认识内格拉时，她七岁。她是一个瘦小的黑皮肤女孩，光着脚，穿着麻袋裙，只有一条内裤。这就是她拥有的全部。她的家里人住在一个单间小屋里，里面有两张床和一个烧木柴的小炉子。我们保持了数十年的联系。我是她其中一个孩子的教父。

00:21:37.490 --> 00:21:55.110
<v Luis Urrea>I had lost Negra seven years ago. Where she lives, without a phone or a street address, it's impossible to stay in contact. Letters can never be delivered except to a nearby grocery store, and then you hope the store owners will give her the letter.
<v Luis Urrea>七年前我失去了内格拉的音讯。在她住的地方，没有电话也没有街道地址，根本无法保持联系。信件永远无法投递，只能送到附近的一家杂货店，然后你只能祈祷店主会把信交给她。

00:21:55.110 --> 00:22:09.060
<v Luis Urrea>We decided to take our boy, Eric. Eric is 14. He has a pierced ear. He wears an MTV Jackass cap. He's entering his first year of high school and he plays the drums.
<v Luis Urrea>我们决定带上我们的男孩埃里克（Eric）。埃里克14岁。他打了耳洞。他戴着MTV《蠢蛋搞怪秀》（Jackass）的帽子。他刚上高一，还会打鼓。

00:22:09.060 --> 00:22:11.780
<v Luis Urrea>[DRUMMING]
<v Luis Urrea>[鼓声]

00:22:11.780 --> 00:22:20.030
<v Ira Glass>Eric. Eric. Eric!
<v Ira Glass>埃里克。埃里克。埃里克！

00:22:20.030 --> 00:22:34.250
<v Eric>Well, before I went down there, I was scared. I actually thought against going. And I have to admit, I cried because I was scared.
<v Eric>嗯，在我去那儿之前，我很害怕。我甚至打消了去的念头。我必须承认，我哭了，因为我很害怕。

00:22:34.250 --> 00:22:39.500
<v Luis Urrea>We live in Chicago, and nothing there could have prepared Eric for Tijuana.
<v Luis Urrea>我们住在芝加哥，那里的一切都无法让埃里克为蒂华纳做好准备。

00:22:39.500 --> 00:22:49.320
<v Luis Urrea>I showed him the neighborhood where I'd been born and the tumbling dirt hill where my grandmother's house had been. And then we headed to the municipal dump.
<v Luis Urrea>我带他看了我出生时的街区，以及我祖母的房子曾经坐落过的那个坍塌的泥土山坡。然后我们前往城市垃圾填埋场。

00:22:49.320 --> 00:23:00.450
<v Eric>It starts out normal, like just a regular city. And then it starts getting weirder and weirder. And then you end up in dirt.
<v Eric>一开始很正常，就像一个普通的城市。然后它开始变得越来越诡异。最后你会发现自己置身于黄土之中。

00:23:00.450 --> 00:23:22.670
<v Luis Urrea>This was all a dirt road where the garbage trucks would come up to bring trash when I worked here. So this whole hillside is now full of auto repair shops and little houses and little restaurants. And we're just getting to where the-- the road just ended, and we hit the rocks.
<v Luis Urrea>这里以前全都是土路，我在这里工作时，垃圾车就是从这里上来运送垃圾的。所以现在整个山坡上到处都是汽车修理厂、小房子和小餐馆。我们正来到——路刚刚到头的地方，然后我们遇到了岩石。

00:23:22.670 --> 00:23:26.730
<v Eric>I saw this one donkey-- burnt. He was all--
<v Eric>我看到这头驴——被烧伤了。它浑身都是——

00:23:26.730 --> 00:23:28.770
<v Luis Urrea>That's mange. It's called mange.
<v Luis Urrea>那是疥癣。这叫疥癣。

00:23:28.770 --> 00:23:32.880
<v Eric>No, I mean he had like little patches where there wasn't any fur--
<v Eric>不，我是说它有几块地方没有毛——

00:23:32.880 --> 00:23:36.600
<v Luis Urrea>That's called mange. That's what I'm telling you. It's a disease they get.
<v Luis Urrea>这就叫疥癣。这就是我告诉你的。这是它们会得的一种病。

00:23:36.600 --> 00:23:39.420
<v Eric>Oh.
<v Eric>哦。

00:23:39.420 --> 00:24:29.390
<v Luis Urrea>At this point, Negra's barrio looks a lot like any other Tijuana neighborhood. Stucco here, cement brick there, mixed in with tumbledown shacks made of plywood, dirt streets. The only sign that it's built on a massive garbage heap is that when it rains, the ground cracks open and gas escapes through small volcanoes. There are garbage mines where neighbors dig shafts in their yards and pull out buried bottles and cans to recycle for money. Street dogs still roam the alleys there. The houses are jury rigged, fences made from bed springs, old mattresses the people pulled from the trash and set afire. Eric had never seen anything like it except in Mad Max movies.
<v Luis Urrea>此时此刻，内格拉的贫民窟看起来跟蒂华纳的其他任何街区都很像。这里有灰泥，那里有水泥砖，夹杂着用胶合板搭建的简陋棚屋和泥泞的街道。它建立在一个巨大的垃圾堆之上的唯一迹象是，当下雨时，地面会裂开，气体通过小火山喷发出来。这里有垃圾矿，邻居们在院子里挖竖井，拉出埋藏的瓶子和罐子回收换钱。野狗仍然在那里的小巷里游荡。这里的房子东拼西凑，栅栏是用弹簧床垫做的，那是人们从垃圾堆里拉出来并点火焚烧的旧床垫。埃里克除了在《疯狂的麦克斯》（Mad Max）电影里之外，从未见过这样的景象。

00:24:29.390 --> 00:24:42.830
<v Luis Urrea>I was worried about Negra. I didn't know what we would find. When I had last seen her, her house was a wooden thing back off the dirt alley. We had built it out of scrap garage doors.
<v Luis Urrea>我当时很担心内格拉。我不知道我们会找到什么。我最后一次见到她时，她的房子是土巷后面的一间木屋。我们是用废弃的车库门拼凑建成的。

00:24:42.830 --> 00:25:11.170
<v Luis Urrea>We drove up the hill and turned down her little street and it looked alien. No one lives in a cardboard box houses anymore. Now there are power poles and telephone lines, lights. Cement walls line the street, built right to the roadway. I stopped a boy and I asked, where does Negra live? He pointed. Right there.
<v Luis Urrea>我们开车上山，拐进她的小巷，那里看起来像外星世界。再也没有人住在纸板箱房子里了。现在这里有了电线杆、电话线和灯光。水泥墙沿着街道排列，直贴着路面。我拦住一个小男孩问：内格拉住在哪里？他指了指。就在那儿。

00:25:11.170 --> 00:25:28.850
<v Luis Urrea>When I pounded on the door, the children let me in, and Negra came running. We were both adults now. As we embraced, she seemed small to me, and I seemed gigantic to her. But as soon as we were together, the intervening years disappeared.
<v Luis Urrea>当我敲门时，孩子们让我进去了，内格拉跑了过来。我们现在都是成年人了。当我们拥抱时，她在我看来显得很小，而我在她看来则庞大无比。但只要我们聚在一起，中间隔著的那些岁月就瞬间消失了。

00:25:28.850 --> 00:25:34.960
<v Negra>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Negra>[说西班牙语]

00:25:34.960 --> 00:26:05.020
<v Translator>I remember the first time my sister introduced him to me. I grabbed Luis's hand and we went to walk in the dump. And I was really happy because I had him by the hand, because I could show him off to all my little friends. "Look! He's my friend! He's my friend!" I was very proud. I began to show him all this stuff-- how to [? order ?] trash, how to collect boxes and sort out bottles, aluminum. I show him how to do it. And I said to my friends, "Look! Luis come to help me!"
<v Translator>我记得我姐姐第一次把他介绍给我的时候。我抓起路易斯的手，我们一起去垃圾场散步。我当时真的很高兴，因为我拉着他的手，因为我可以向我所有的好朋友炫耀。“看！他是我的朋友！他是我的朋友！”我感到非常骄傲。我开始向他展示所有这些东西——如何[？分类？]垃圾，如何收集纸盒、挑选瓶子和铝。我教他怎么做。我对朋友们说：“看！路易斯来帮我了！”

00:26:05.020 --> 00:26:59.280
<v Luis Urrea>Negra used to come running when we showed up, calling my name from far out in the trash. I would come to this apocalyptic scene on a hillside above Tijuana amidst wrecked cars, piles of trash, paper shacks, and sometimes towers of dead animals set afire. I would hear this little voice in the racket, calling me. "Luis! Luis! Luis!" She would leap out of the trash and fly like a little bird and land against my chest, small-boned and tough and sinewy. And she would say, "Give me a piggyback ride." And I'd carry her through the garbage.
<v Luis Urrea>每当我们出现时，内格拉总是跑过来，在蒂华纳上方山坡的垃圾堆深处大声喊着我的名字。我来到蒂华纳上方山坡上的这个末日般的场景中，这里到处都是损毁的汽车、堆积如山的垃圾、纸糊的棚屋，有时还有燃烧着的动物尸体塔。我会在喧嚣中听到这个小小的声音在呼唤我。“路易斯！路易斯！路易斯！”她会从垃圾堆里跳出来，像一只小鸟一样飞扑过来，落在我胸前，骨骼纤细、坚韧而有力。然后她会说：“背我骑马。”于是我背着她穿过垃圾堆。

00:26:59.280 --> 00:27:22.630
<v Luis Urrea>So now here's the situation that her man, Jaime, my compadre, has apparently been sort of macho and bad, and gotten a girlfriend or two or three or four. And Negra has sent him packing. So now she's got a house full of women in a very tough neighborhood. Girls.
<v Luis Urrea>所以现在的情况是，她的男人、我的老朋友海梅（Jaime），显然变得有点大男子主义和混蛋，找了一个或两个或三个或四个女朋友。而内格拉已经把他给踢走了。所以现在她在这个非常艰难的街区里，独自抚养一屋子的女性。女孩们。

00:27:22.630 --> 00:27:45.540
<v Luis Urrea>Her sister was beaten by her husband in a drug frenzy and sustained some brain damage that ultimately killed her. So her sister Maria has died. And Negra has now inherited Maria's three daughters.
<v Luis Urrea>她的姐姐在丈夫的一场吸毒狂欢中被殴打，头部受到了一些最终致命的损伤。所以她的姐姐玛丽亚（Maria）已经去世了。而内格拉现在继承了玛丽亚的三个女儿。

00:27:45.540 --> 00:28:05.320
<v Luis Urrea>So Negra has gone from having three girls on her own to having six girls on her own. So at the age of 31, she's suddenly the mom of girls ranging from eight years old to 19 years old.
<v Luis Urrea>因此，内格拉从自己抚养三个女孩变成了独自抚养六个女孩。所以在31岁的时候，她突然成了一群年龄从8岁到19岁不等的女孩们的妈妈。

00:28:05.320 --> 00:28:25.590
<v Luis Urrea>As it turned out, we had shown up just in time for the graduation of Negra's niece, Blanca. Negra called me upstairs and I ascended into a world of lace and perfume and clouds of Mexican hair. I said, Eric is going to faint. Somehow this comment began the miracle.
<v Luis Urrea>事实证明，我们出现得正是时候，正好赶上了内格拉的侄女布兰卡（Blanca）的毕业典礼。内格拉叫我上楼，我走进了一个充满蕾丝、香水和墨西哥发型云雾的世界。我说，埃里克快要晕倒了。不知怎的，这句话开启了奇迹。

00:28:25.590 --> 00:28:50.010
<v Luis Urrea>I went back down and sat with him. He looked lost in the scrap couch, all alone, blushing with fear. Then the angels began to descend, and they did it with a kind of natural drama our Latina women know. They came down by age, youngest first, and they walked with grace, as if they were all in the graduation ceremony.
<v Luis Urrea>我走回楼下和他坐在一起。他孤零零地陷在那个破沙发里，看上去不知所措，害羞得满脸通红。接着，天使们开始降临了，她们带着一种我们拉美女性所熟知的天然戏剧感走了下来。她们按年龄走下来，最小的打头阵，仪态万千，仿佛她们全都在参加毕业典礼。

00:28:50.010 --> 00:29:12.240
<v Luis Urrea>In Eric's world, the chicks flounce into a room and toss off a nod and mutter, "What's up?" In Negra's house, each girl came downstairs, walked to Eric, and kissed him. Whoa, dude. His ears were bright red.
<v Luis Urrea>在埃里克的世界里，辣妹们扭着腰走进房间，点点头，咕哝着说：“怎么了？”但在内格拉的家里，每个女孩都下楼走到埃里克跟前，吻了他。哇，老兄。他的耳朵红得发亮。

00:29:12.240 --> 00:29:38.190
<v Eric>Yes, they kissed me on the cheek. I probably was blood red. I know I was red. For sure I was red. And I didn't know what to expect, you know? It was-- I don't know how to describe it.
<v Eric>是的，她们亲吻了我的脸颊。我的脸可能红得像血一样。我知道自己红了。我敢肯定我红了。我当时不知道会发生什么，你知道吗？那简直是——我不知道该怎么形容。

00:29:38.190 --> 00:29:51.430
<v Luis Urrea>"Nayeli" is a word that means "flower of the household." When I last saw her, she was a tiny seven year old. She was Negra's eldest daughter, now a somber and beautiful young woman of 16.
<v Luis Urrea>“娜耶莉”（Nayeli）这个词的意思是“家庭之花”。我最后一次见到她时，她是个七岁的小不点。她是内格拉的长女，现在则是一个忧郁而美丽的16岁年轻姑娘。

00:29:51.430 --> 00:29:56.950
<v Nayeli>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Nayeli>[说西班牙语]

00:29:56.950 --> 00:30:06.990
<v Translator>Eric arrived, and I was like, ay, he seems so timid, so serious. He was stuck like glue to Luis, just stuck to him. So if Luis moved over there, Eric went too.
<v Translator>埃里克来了，我想，哎呀，他看起来好羞怯，好严肃。他像胶水一样粘着路易斯，就粘在他身上。所以如果路易斯挪到那边，埃里克也跟着过去。

00:30:06.990 --> 00:30:33.130
<v Luis Urrea>The 10-year-old kissed him. The 11-year-old kissed him. Then-- uh oh. The 14-year-old kissed him. The redness spread from his ears to his cheeks. The gorgeous 15-year-old graduate, in her lavender gown, descended and kissed him. He started to giggle. She sat on the arm of the couch near him and he sat up straight.
<v Luis Urrea>10岁的女孩亲了他。11岁的女孩亲了他。然后——噢哦。14岁的女孩亲了他。红晕从他的耳朵蔓延到脸颊。那个穿着薰衣草长裙的美丽15岁毕业生走下来亲了他。他开始咯咯傻笑。她坐在他附近沙发的扶手上，他挺直了腰板。

00:30:33.130 --> 00:30:35.960
<v Nayeli>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Nayeli>[说西班牙语]

00:30:35.960 --> 00:30:43.540
<v Translator>It was probably his first experience like that, with those kisses we gave him. And that's why he got so embarrassed.
<v Translator>这可能是他第一次经历这种事，接受我们给他的那些吻。这就是为什么他会这么尴尬。

00:30:43.540 --> 00:30:54.250
<v Luis Urrea>Then Nayeli, all in black, looking 25 in a belly button Shakira outfit, came down and stared at him, then kissed him.
<v Luis Urrea>然后娜耶莉一身黑衣走下来，穿着露肚脐的夏奇拉（Shakira）风格服装，看起来像25岁，她盯着他看了一会儿，然后亲了他。

00:30:54.250 --> 00:31:15.320
<v Luis Urrea>Finally the 19-year-old, in Shakira's other outfit, with blue eyelids, came down and said things in Spanish that he didn't understand, and kissed him, and put her hand on his face. He says he didn't wet his pants.
<v Luis Urrea>最后，那个19岁的女孩穿着夏奇拉风格的另一套衣服、画着蓝色眼影走下来，用他听不懂的西班牙语说了一些话，亲了他，并把手放在他的脸上。他说他没有尿裤子。

00:31:15.320 --> 00:31:34.520
<v Eric>They started playing-- I think it was Eminem. They started playing Eminem, and they're like, you know this? And then, well, I'd rather not talk about it.
<v Eric>他们开始放音乐——我想那是埃米纳姆（Eminem）。他们开始放埃米纳姆的歌，然后他们说，你知道这个吗？然后，嗯，我还是不谈这个了。

00:31:34.520 --> 00:32:16.290
<v Eric>Well, I was with my mom, and my mom was standing next to me. And Martha, the 19-year-old, she starts pointing at me, and I'm like, what's going on here? I have my bear claw necklace on and I hold it up, and I'm like, this? And she's like, no. And she grabs her belt buckle and says, "belt buckle, belt buckle." And then her and Nayeli start giggling. And my mom's, like, looking at my shirt. And then she says-- Martha says-- "I don't know how to say it!"
<v Eric>嗯，我和我妈妈在一起，我妈妈站在我旁边。19岁的玛莎（Martha）开始指着我，我就想，这里发生什么事了？我戴着熊爪项链，把它举起来，心想：是这个吗？然后她说：不是。接着她抓住自己的皮带扣说：“皮带扣，皮带扣。”然后她和娜耶莉开始咯咯笑。我妈妈呢，看着我的衬衫。然后她说——玛莎说——“我不知道该怎么说！”

00:32:16.290 --> 00:32:34.330
<v Eric>And then my mom looks down. And my fly was open! You know, I'm standing there, like, hey, check out my bear claw! Is this what you're talking about? Yeah, this is cool, huh? And then my fly was open!
<v Eric>然后我妈妈往下看。我的裤子拉链没拉！你知道的，我站在那里，就像：嘿，看看我的熊爪！你们说的是这个吗？对，这个很酷，对吧？然后我的裤子拉链没拉！

00:32:34.330 --> 00:32:46.940
<v Luis Urrea>The girls laughed, but it wasn't cruel. His haplessness won them all over at once, and he became the cheerful fiance to the entire crowd. They began to fight for his attention.
<v Luis Urrea>女孩们笑了，但并不恶意。他那笨拙的模样一下子赢得了所有人的喜爱，他成了全场瞩目的开心果。她们开始争夺对他的注意力。

00:32:46.940 --> 00:32:50.790
<v Nayeli>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Nayeli>[说西班牙语]

00:32:50.790 --> 00:33:05.200
<v Translator>What did I think of Eric? I thought, he's still just a kid. He's just a kid. That was my first reaction. A kid. And it's not every day that he's surrounded by girls who are hugging him, and grabbing his hand, and giving him kisses on the cheek.
<v Translator>我对埃里克的看法？我觉得，他依然只是个孩子。他只是个孩子。那是我的第一反应。一个孩子。而且他不是每天都能被一群女孩包围，她们拥抱他、拉他的手、亲吻他的脸颊。

00:33:05.200 --> 00:33:29.360
<v Luis Urrea>If he didn't flirt with the 16-year-old, she'd pout, while the 14-year-old fumed because the 19-year-old put her arm through his. "Daddy," he said, "nobody back home is going to believe this." There was a pause. "Please, take some pictures."
<v Luis Urrea>如果他没有对那个16岁的女孩调情，她就会撅起嘴，而14岁的女孩则会生闷气，因为19岁的女孩把手臂挽進了他的胳膊里。“爸爸，”他说，“老家没有人会相信这一幕的。”停顿了一下。“求你了，拍几张照片吧。”

00:33:29.360 --> 00:33:38.090
<v Luis Urrea>Now, were the girls holding you and hugging you and stuff? I seem to remember, they were holding onto you pretty tight.
<v Luis Urrea>那么，女孩子们当时有拉着你、拥抱你什么的吗？我好像记得，她们把你抓得挺紧的。

00:33:38.090 --> 00:33:47.010
<v Eric>Yep. I guess I was like-- a Teddy bear.
<v Eric>是的。我想我当时就像个泰迪熊。

00:33:47.010 --> 00:34:21.800
<v Eric>It was weird. Because you know, if I had met those people in nicer conditions, I would never have guessed they were poor at all. I don't know how to describe it. I guess they could still maintain their dignity. It was just so weird how they could do that. Like, if I were them, I couldn't do that at all.
<v Eric>这感觉很诡异。因为你知道，如果我在更好的条件下遇到这些人，我绝对不会猜到他们很穷。我不知道该怎么形容。我想他们依然能保持自己的尊严。她们怎么能做到这一点，真是太诡异了。就像，如果换作是我，我绝对做不到。

00:34:21.800 --> 00:34:55.000
<v Luis Urrea>We were all together, and in classic Mexican fashion, it was time to eat. Eric was nervous about the meal, since he's a picky eater at the best of times. And here we were in this neighborhood that's not terribly hygienic. He had no way of knowing that everything around him-- the couch, the table, the plates and silverware and pans-- had all been scavenged out of the garbage. I decided not to share that information at the moment.
<v Luis Urrea>我们聚在一起，按照经典的墨西哥风俗，该吃饭了。埃里克对这顿饭感到紧张，因为他在平时就是一个挑食的人。而我们现在身处这个卫生条件不怎么样的街区。他无从得知周围的一切——沙发、桌子、盘子、餐具和平底锅——全都是从垃圾堆里捡来的。我决定暂时不告诉他这个信息。

00:34:55.000 --> 00:35:13.340
<v Luis Urrea>They were going to cook us carne asada. I told him it was safe to eat the cooked meat and the tortillas, but the boiled beans or the salsa might make him sick. He nodded. His eyes were huge, but he smiled at Nayeli as if he couldn't wait to dig in.
<v Luis Urrea>她们打算给我们做烤肉（carne asada）。我告诉他吃烤肉和玉米饼是安全的，但水煮豆子或莎莎酱可能会让他生病。他点点头。他的眼睛睁得老大，但他对娜耶莉微笑，仿佛已经迫不及待地想要开动了。

00:35:13.340 --> 00:35:22.840
<v Luis Urrea>The girls had to go to the store to buy charcoal. They refused to go without Eric. They snagged his arms and vanished.
<v Luis Urrea>女孩们得去商店买木炭。她们拒绝不带埃里克一起去。她们抓住他的胳膊，一溜烟不见了。

00:35:22.840 --> 00:35:36.730
<v Luis Urrea>At the store, they didn't have enough money to buy a bag of charcoal. They bought six briquettes. The woman in the store had the bag cut open, and they counted out six and wrapped them in a piece of newspaper.
<v Luis Urrea>到了商店，她们的钱不够买一整袋木炭。她们买了一块炭的六个小块。店里的女人把袋子剪开，她们数出六块，用一张报纸包起来。

00:35:36.730 --> 00:35:46.520
<v Luis Urrea>It was such a gift those girls gave Eric. They held him, but more importantly, they held him publicly. They made a show of him.
<v Luis Urrea>那些女孩给了埃里克多么珍贵的礼物啊。她们拥抱了他，但更重要的是，她们公开地拥抱了他。她们对他表现出了大方的喜爱。

00:35:46.520 --> 00:36:19.550
<v Luis Urrea>When I was a boy, unloved and unlovely, unpopular and scared to walk in the neighborhood, my teenaged cousin Margarita came to live with us. And when we walked down the street, she would hold my hand so everyone could see us. And when my enemies came in sight, she would lace her fingers through mine as if she were my girlfriend. As if she loved me so much, she needed to hold me even tighter.
<v Luis Urrea>当我还是个男孩时，无人疼爱、不够可爱、既不讨人喜欢又害怕在这个街区走动，我的表姐玛格丽塔（Margarita）搬来和我们同住。当我们走在街上时，她会拉着我的手，好让所有人都能看见我们。每当我看到仇敌出现时，她就会把手指扣进我的指缝里，就像她是我的女朋友一样。就好像她那么爱我，以至于需要把我抓得更紧。

00:36:19.550 --> 00:36:22.460
<v Announcer>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Announcer>[说西班牙语]

00:36:22.460 --> 00:36:37.360
<v Luis Urrea>The graduation was at the John F. Kennedy and Juan [UNINTELLIGIBLE] Academy. Our car became the shuttle running 100 people up and down the hill. The faculty of the school sat an old record player on a folding chair.
<v Luis Urrea>毕业典礼在约瑟夫·F·肯尼迪和胡安[听不清]学院举行。我们的汽车成了运送100人上下山坡的穿梭巴士。学校的教职员工把一台旧唱片机放在一把折叠椅上。

00:36:37.360 --> 00:37:04.580
<v Luis Urrea>All the kids were in their uniforms, and the girls were in their inexpensive taffeta gowns. The theme was lilac. Each girl also had a small cotton stole. They all had hairdos, and they wore really painful-looking high heels, looking as beautiful as possible. The small kids were in their uniforms, and the kindergarten kids were dressed in cowboy suits.
<v Luis Urrea>所有的孩子都穿着制服，女孩们穿着便宜的塔夫绸长礼服。主题是丁香紫。每个女孩还披着一条小棉质披肩。她们都做好了发型，穿着看起来非常痛苦的高跟鞋，竭尽全力展现美丽。小孩子们穿着校服，幼儿园的孩子们则穿着牛仔服。

00:37:04.580 --> 00:37:16.460
<v Luis Urrea>The school principal first led everyone in applause for the teachers who heroically talked and the guests of honor, who were teachers from other schools, stood and got rounds of ferocious applause.
<v Luis Urrea>校长首先带头为那些辛勤教导的老师们鼓掌，作为特邀嘉宾的其他学校老师们起立，获得了雷鸣般热烈的掌声。

00:37:16.460 --> 00:37:42.460
<v Luis Urrea>And they started playing, if I remember right, really bad old Stevie Wonder records. Each class did dances to Stevie Wonder, and all the ninth graders did a waltz to it. Each group marched out and did a dance in the rubble.
<v Luis Urrea>然后他们开始放音乐，如果我没记错的话，是非常糟糕的史提夫·旺达（Stevie Wonder）老唱片。每个班级都伴着史提夫·旺达的音乐跳舞，所有九年级的学生都跳了一支华尔兹。每个小组都在废墟中列队出来跳了一支舞。

00:37:42.460 --> 00:37:53.810
<v Luis Urrea>Did San Diego and the United States look different to you after this experience? When we came back, did you feel like you were seeing it through new eyes? Because didn't we go right to Disneyland after that?
<v Luis Urrea>经历这次之后，圣迭戈和美国在你眼里变得不一样了吗？当我们回来时，你觉得你是用全新的眼光看待这一切吗？因为我们不是在那之后直接去了迪士尼乐园吗？

00:37:53.810 --> 00:38:18.060
<v Eric>Yeah, we did go to Disneyland after that. Once we got back there, you know, the whole trip there seemed unreal. Like it never really happened. You know? It felt like a dream to me, because it was just so unbelievably strange. Never in a million years would I have guessed I'd be going there.
<v Eric>是的，我们在那之后确实去了迪士尼乐园。一旦我们回到那里，你知道，那整趟旅程看起来就像是不真实的。就像从来没有真正发生过一样。你知道吗？这对我来说感觉像一场梦，因为它实在太不可思议地诡异了。我做梦也没想到自己会去那种地方。

00:38:18.060 --> 00:38:19.590
<v Luis Urrea>Would you go back?
<v Luis Urrea>你还会再去吗？

00:38:19.590 --> 00:38:20.470
<v Eric>Yeah.
<v Eric>会。

00:38:20.470 --> 00:38:21.800
<v Luis Urrea>You would go back?
<v Luis Urrea>你还会去？

00:38:21.800 --> 00:38:32.330
<v Eric>Yes. That would be a definite yes. I would go back.
<v Eric>是的。答案是肯定的。我会回去的。

00:38:32.330 --> 00:38:56.700
<v Ira Glass>Luis Alberto Urrea is the author of several books of fiction and nonfiction, including By the Lake of the Sleeping Children, The Secret Life of the Mexican Border, and Across the Wire. This story was produced by Barbara Ferry with help from Sandy Tolan, Alan Weisman, and Deborah Begel. It's part of the series "Border Stories" from Homelands Productions, which gets funds from the Ford Foundation and the Corporation for Public Broadcasting.
<v Ira Glass>路易斯·阿尔贝托·乌雷亚是多部小说和非小说作品的作者，包括《沉睡儿童的湖畔》、《墨西哥边境的秘密生活》和《跨越铁丝网》。这个故事由芭芭拉·费瑞（Barbara Ferry）制作，并得到了桑迪·托兰（Sandy Tolan）、艾伦·韦斯曼（Alan Weisman）和黛博拉·贝格尔（Deborah Begel）的帮助。它是家园制作公司（Homelands Productions）“边境故事”系列的一部分，该系列资金来源于福特基金会和公共广播公司。

00:38:56.700 --> 00:39:17.590
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "TRASH" BY THE NEW YORK DOLLS]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 纽约玩偶乐队的《垃圾》]

00:39:17.590 --> 00:39:23.340
<v Ira Glass>Act Three. I'm a Legitimate Businessman... In Waste Management.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕。我是一个合法的商人……废弃物管理公司。

00:39:23.340 --> 00:39:39.630
<v Ira Glass>Starting in the mid-1950s, private trash collection in New York was controlled by the mob. Private trash collection, you should understand, means anything that the city won't pick up, which in New York, means any trash that is generated by any business. A restaurant, an office building, a car shop, anything at all.
<v Ira Glass>从20世纪50年代中期开始，纽约的私人垃圾收集业务一直由黑帮控制。你应该明白，私人垃圾收集指的是任何城市拒绝收集的东西，在纽约，这意味着任何企业产生的任何垃圾。餐馆、办公楼、汽车修理厂，任何东西都可以。

00:39:39.630 --> 00:40:09.300
<v Ira Glass>As you might expect, in New York, that is a lot of money. $1.5 billion a year back in 1995, which is when this next story takes place. For 50 years, everybody, including city officials, knew that the mob was running a garbage cartel, driving up prices all over the city. But in the mid-1990s, the entire operation was brought down more or less by one man-- an NYPD detective named Rick Cowan who went undercover for three years, making hundreds of hours of secret recordings.
<v Ira Glass>正如你所料，在纽约，这是一笔巨款。早在1995年，也就是接下来这个故事发生的时候，每年就有15亿美元。50年来，每个人，包括城市官员，都知道黑帮在经营一个垃圾托拉斯（卡特尔），推高了整个城市的垃圾处理价格。但在20世纪90年代中期，整个犯罪网络或多或少被一个人摧毁了——他是一名名叫里克·科恩（Rick Cowan）的纽约市警察局侦探，他卧底三年，录下了数百小时的秘密录音。

00:40:09.300 --> 00:40:30.320
<v Ira Glass>Now before this, Cowen had gone undercover in the narcotics division, but never for more than two weeks. And he had no intention of starting a historic, city-wide undercover sting operation when our story begins. When our story begins, he's a guy in the NYPD Organized Crime division. He's investigating mafia loan sharking in the garbage business. And he just catches a lucky break.
<v Ira Glass>在此之前，科恩曾在缉毒部门卧底，但时间从未超过两周。当我们的故事开始时，他根本无意发起一场史无前例的全市范围卧底钓鱼执法行动。故事开始时，他是纽约市警察局有组织犯罪部门的一名警员。他正在调查垃圾行业中的黑手党高利贷。然后他走运了。

00:40:30.320 --> 00:40:59.450
<v Ira Glass>It's a truck explosion. A truck owned by a recycling company called Chambers Paper Fiber, was blown up underneath the Manhattan Bridge in Brooklyn. When Detective Cowen gets there, the owner of the company-- a guy named Sal Benedetto-- tells him that he'd been having trouble with the mob. That the second largest trash company in the city, a mob-run company, had taken his biggest customer, so he turned around and took one of the mob's customers. Ever since then, his drivers had been threatened, and now this truck was blown up.
<v Ira Glass>发生了一起卡车爆炸案。一辆属于一家名为“钱伯斯纸业纤维”（Chambers Paper Fiber）的回收公司的卡车，在布鲁克林曼哈顿大桥下被炸毁。当科恩侦探赶到时，公司的老板——一个名叫萨尔·贝内代托（Sal Benedetto）的人——告诉他，他一直遭到黑帮的骚扰。市内第二大垃圾公司、一家由黑帮运营的公司抢走了他最大的客户，于是他反戈一击，抢走了黑帮的一个客户。从那以后，他的司机一直受到威胁，现在这辆卡车又被炸毁了。

00:40:59.450 --> 00:41:03.150
<v Ira Glass>Detective Cowen remembers meeting Sal Benedetto this way.
<v Ira Glass>科恩侦探是这样回忆与萨尔·贝内代托相遇的：

00:41:03.150 --> 00:41:25.050
<v Rick Cowen>Very personable guy. And 15 minutes into the interview, his shop foreman interrupted us and said, Sal, the guy that burned the truck just drove by. So we thought that maybe we could get outside and follow the car, or see the car, and get the license plate, you know, we'd have possibly information and evidence that we could lead for an arrest.
<v Rick Cowen>非常随和的一个人。采访进行了15分钟后，他的车间领班打断了我们，说道：“萨尔，烧卡车的那个人刚刚开车从门口经过。”所以我们想，也许我们可以出去跟着那辆车，或者看看那辆车，记下车牌号，你知道的，我们可能会有线索和证据，从而顺藤摸瓜实施逮捕。

00:41:25.050 --> 00:41:54.060
<v Rick Cowen>So unbeknownst to us, as I was rushing out-- I was the first one to run out the door-- these two guys were coming in. You know, I startled them, they startled me, and I stopped right in my tracks. This one guy was built like a big football player. The second guy had his hand in his pocket and pointed as if he had a gun. The larger of the two was doing all the talking. He asked who I was, and with no rhyme or reason, the owner, Sal Benedetto, says, "That's cousin Dan.  He works here." Or something to that effect.
<v Rick Cowen>所以在我们不知情的情况下，当我冲出去的时候——我是第一个冲出门的人——这两个家伙正往里走。你知道，我吓了他们一跳，他们也吓了我一跳，我当场就停下了脚步。这其中一个人身材魁梧得像个橄榄球运动员。第二个人把手插在口袋里，做出一副有枪的手势。两人中体型较大的那个一直在说话。他问我是谁，毫无逻辑和理由地，老板萨尔·贝内代托脱口而出：“这是堂弟丹。他在我们这儿工作。”或者类似的话。

00:41:54.060 --> 00:41:57.270
<v Ira Glass>So Sal just instinctively just gives you a cover.
<v Ira Glass>所以萨尔本能地就给你安了个掩护身份。

00:41:57.270 --> 00:42:04.110
<v Rick Cowen>Yeah. Calling the cops in this business is a death sentence. It's just not done.
<v Rick Cowen>对。在这个行业里报警等于判死刑。根本没人这么干。

00:42:04.110 --> 00:42:15.390
<v Ira Glass>Mob guys want to know if the Benedettos are going to give up the customer that they just stole from the mob. And Detective Cowen, aka their brand new cousin Danny, finds himself in the middle of this discussion.
<v Ira Glass>黑帮分子想知道贝内代托家族是否打算交出他们刚从黑帮那里抢来的客户。科恩侦探，也就是他们的全新堂弟丹尼，发现自己卷入了这场讨论的中心。

00:42:15.390 --> 00:42:33.780
<v Rick Cowen>And this guy said that there was going to be a sit down over this, and he demand to know, by two o'clock, what our intentions were. And he gave me a business card with a phone number on the back and wanted to be called at that number. And that's what we did.
<v Rick Cowen>这个家伙说，为此将有一场闭门谈判，他要求在两点前知道我们的意向。他给了我一张名片，背面写着一个电话号码，并要求打那个电话找他。我们就这么做了。

00:42:33.780 --> 00:42:34.860
<v Raymond>Hello?
<v Raymond>喂？

00:42:34.860 --> 00:42:36.210
<v Rick Cowen>Hello. Are you the Raymond?
<v Rick Cowen>喂。你是雷蒙德吗？

00:42:36.210 --> 00:42:37.080
<v Raymond>Yeah, this is Raymond.
<v Raymond>对，我是雷蒙德。

00:42:37.080 --> 00:42:39.070
<v Rick Cowen>This is Danny of Chambers Paper.
<v Rick Cowen>我是钱伯斯纸业的丹尼。

00:42:39.070 --> 00:42:40.740
<v Raymond>Yeah. I saw you.
<v Raymond>对。我看到你了。

00:42:40.740 --> 00:42:42.540
<v Rick Cowen>I saw you today with Sal.
<v Rick Cowen>我今天和萨尔一起看到你了。

00:42:42.540 --> 00:42:43.310
<v Raymond>Right.
<v Raymond>没错。

00:42:43.310 --> 00:42:43.660
<v Rick Cowen>Yeah.
<v Rick Cowen>对。

00:42:43.660 --> 00:42:47.020
<v Raymond>And then you guys started getting stupid and started following me.
<v Raymond>然后你们就开始犯傻，开始跟踪我。

00:42:47.020 --> 00:42:50.420
<v Rick Cowen>No, we were leaving. We saw you backing up the [UNINTELLIGIBLE] down the street--
<v Rick Cowen>不，我们要离开了。我们看到你把[听不清]倒着开下街道——

00:42:50.420 --> 00:42:51.730
<v Raymond>I'm not stupid.
<v Raymond>我可不傻。

00:42:51.730 --> 00:42:53.860
<v Rick Cowen>Listen. I'm not stupid either. Listen--
<v Rick Cowen>听着。我也不傻。听着——

00:42:53.860 --> 00:42:58.200
<v Ira Glass>Now he's accusing you of following him. Did you guys follow him after you left the place?
<v Ira Glass>现在他指责你跟踪他。你们离开那地方之后真的跟踪他了吗？

00:42:58.200 --> 00:43:04.600
<v Raymond>Yeah. We followed him-- and they obviously saw us, and they looped around, and we let them go at that point.
<v Raymond>对。我们跟着他——他们显然看到我们了，然后他们绕了个圈，我们在那个时候就让他们走了。

00:43:04.600 --> 00:43:07.930
<v Rick Cowen>We would like to sit down and get together.
<v Rick Cowen>我们想坐下来聚一聚。

00:43:07.930 --> 00:43:08.710
<v Raymond>Next week?
<v Raymond>下周？

00:43:08.710 --> 00:43:10.760
<v Rick Cowen>Yes. Next week.
<v Rick Cowen>对。下周。

00:43:10.760 --> 00:43:13.100
<v Raymond>All right.
<v Raymond>好吧。

00:43:13.100 --> 00:43:15.530
<v Rick Cowen>You know, can you come over here?
<v Rick Cowen>你知道，你能过来这里吗？

00:43:15.530 --> 00:43:16.420
<v Raymond>When?
<v Raymond>什么时候？

00:43:16.420 --> 00:43:18.640
<v Rick Cowen>No, I mean-- no, next week.
<v Rick Cowen>不，我是说——不，下周。

00:43:18.640 --> 00:43:20.480
<v Raymond>Next week you want to do it?
<v Raymond>你想下周办这事？

00:43:20.480 --> 00:43:21.570
<v Rick Cowen>Right.
<v Rick Cowen>对。

00:43:21.570 --> 00:43:27.190
<v Ira Glass>In this tape, you sound a little bit nervous. You can hear your voice shaking. You can hear you breathing while he's talking.
<v Ira Glass>在这个磁带里，听起来你有点紧张。你能听到你的声音在颤抖。当他说话时，你能听到你的呼吸声。

00:43:27.190 --> 00:43:36.290
<v Rick Cowen>Well, I was very nervous making this phone call, no doubt about it. I knew nothing about the garbage business other than what I was told since I was a little kid, that it was all mob-controlled.
<v Rick Cowen>嗯，打这通电话时我确实非常紧张，毫无疑问。我对垃圾行业一无所知，除了从小听到的那些传闻——整个行业都被黑帮控制。

00:43:36.290 --> 00:43:38.610
<v Rick Cowen>We will sit down like gentlemen, and we--
<v Rick Cowen>我们会像绅士一样坐下来，然后我们——

00:43:38.610 --> 00:43:41.260
<v Raymond>No problem. That's what I want. That's what I want.
<v Raymond>没问题。这正是我想要的。这正是我想要的。

00:43:41.260 --> 00:43:46.040
<v Rick Cowen>But you know, I mean, I'll meet you halfway. I mean--
<v Rick Cowen>但你知道，我的意思是，我会做出妥协。我的意思是——

00:43:46.040 --> 00:43:52.620
<v Raymond>I'll meet you halfway if you meet me halfway. The only thing I want is for us to sit down and discuss this like men.
<v Raymond>如果你对我让步，我也会对你让步。我唯一的要求就是我们坐下来像个男人一样讨论这件事。

00:43:52.620 --> 00:44:04.700
<v Rick Cowen>We weren't assuming that this was going to lead to a big undercover operation. This was just some thug that came down to Chambers that day.
<v Rick Cowen>我们当时并没有假设这会演变成一场声势浩大的卧底行动。那天到钱伯斯来的不过是个暴徒而已。

00:44:04.700 --> 00:44:25.030
<v Ira Glass>At best, the cops thought this guy might name some higher up in the mob who they could then drag in. But one conversation with the mobsters led to a second, and third, and pretty soon, the cops decided to turn the Irish Rick Cowen into the Italian Danny Benedetto, cousin Danny from Staten Island, full time, to see just how far it could go.
<v Ira Glass>往最好的方面想，警察认为这家伙可能会供出黑帮内部更高层的人物，然后他们就可以把那些人抓起来。但与黑帮分子的第一次对话引出了第二次、第三次，很快，警方决定让爱尔兰裔的里克·科恩全职变身成意大利裔的丹尼·贝内代托——来自斯塔滕岛的堂弟丹尼，看看这能走多远。

00:44:25.030 --> 00:44:49.170
<v Ira Glass>The NYPD set him up with a fake ID, an SUV like the SUVs that guys in the garbage business drove, and a dummy apartment in a high rise building on Staten Island where a lot of Italians lived. Every day, Rick would drive from his real home, sneak into that building, get the SUV, and then drive to work at the Benedetto family recycling business, Chambers Papers, where Sal Benedetto gave him a full time job.
<v Ira Glass>纽约市警察局为他伪造了身份证件，配了一辆垃圾行业内的人常开的那种SUV，并在斯塔滕岛一栋住着很多意大利人的高层公寓里租了一间假公寓。每天，里克从自己真正的家出发，潜入那栋大楼，开上SUV，然后开车去贝内代托家族的回收企业钱伯斯纸业上班，萨尔·贝内代托在那里给他安排了一份全职工作。

00:44:49.170 --> 00:45:10.440
<v Ira Glass>Because some of the mob guys knew the Benedetto family going back generations-- some of them had actually come from the same town in Italy-- Rick had to learn the entire family tree of this huge family, which was kind of a nightmare, actually. He also had to learn from Sal everything about the recycled paper business. The processes, the grades of paper.
<v Ira Glass>由于一些黑帮分子几代人前就认识贝内代托家族——其中一些人甚至来自意大利的同一个小镇——里克必须背熟这个庞大家族的整个族谱，这实际上简直是一场噩梦。他还必须向萨尔学习关于再生纸业务的一切。各种流程、纸张的等级。

00:45:10.440 --> 00:45:20.800
<v Rick Cowen>The paper recycling business had 25 different paper grades. Single white ledgers, soft color ledger, mixed office waste, CPO groundwood, CPO laser free. Things like this.
<v Rick Cowen>纸张回收业务有25种不同的纸张等级。单面白ledger、软彩ledger、混合办公室废纸、CPO地木纸、CPO无激光纸。诸如此类。

00:45:20.800 --> 00:45:30.090
<v Rick Cowen>Any equipment that went along with it. I mean, if-- and this happens later on in the case, where a guy would say, hey, what kind of [UNINTELLIGIBLE] are you running in your shop? I mean, I had to know these things.
<v Rick Cowen>以及与之配套的任何设备。我的意思是，如果——案情后来发展到这一步，有人会说，嘿，你在车间里开的是什么[听不清]设备？我的意思是，我必须知道这些事情。

00:45:30.090 --> 00:45:31.140
<v Ira Glass>Man.
<v Ira Glass>天哪。

00:45:31.140 --> 00:45:52.350
<v Rick Cowen>But I would have coffee with Sal around 10 or 10:30 in the morning, and when we'd go out, we'd talk about things like this. And Sal knew the business, and it was almost like he could do it in his sleep, but he couldn't articulate it to me very well. But eventually, I learned enough to pull it off like people who had been in the business for generations.
<v Rick Cowen>但我会在早上10点或10点半左右和萨尔一起喝咖啡，当我们出去的时候，我们会谈论这些事情。萨尔对这行了如指掌，做起来简直闭着眼睛都能干，但他很难向我清晰地表达出来。不过最终，我学到了足够的知识，能够像那些祖祖辈辈都在这行里的人一样把它混过去。

00:45:52.350 --> 00:45:56.410
<v Ira Glass>How much danger was the Benedetto family putting itself in to let you all do this?
<v Ira Glass>让你们所有人做这件事，贝内代托家族把自己置于多大的危险之中？

00:45:56.410 --> 00:46:15.200
<v Rick Cowen>A considerable amount of danger. Certainly Sal. Sal told people in the industry that I was his second cousin. He could have been killed.
<v Rick Cowen>相当大的危险。尤其是萨尔。萨尔告诉业内人士我是他的堂弟。他可能会被杀掉。

00:46:15.200 --> 00:46:28.020
<v Ira Glass>The way the sting worked was this. While Rick was working at Chambers Papers, he got himself involved in several turf wars with the mob. One of these actually involved the company that produces the number one mob TV series in America, HBO.
<v Ira Glass>这场钓鱼执法的运作方式是这样的。当里克在钱伯斯纸业工作时，他让自己卷入了与黑帮的几场势力范围争夺战。其中一起甚至牵涉到美国头号黑帮电视剧制作公司——HBO。

00:46:28.020 --> 00:46:46.710
<v Ira Glass>HBO headquarters in New York had their paper recycling hauled out by a company that happened to be controlled by the mob-- like most haulers were. Nothing unusual about that. Sal Benedetto's company, Chambers, bid for their garbage collection business. And because their bid was lower than the mob-run company, they got the job, taking it from the mobsters.
<v Ira Glass>HBO在纽约的总部的纸张回收业务是由一家恰好被黑帮控制的公司承揽的——就像大多数垃圾运输商一样。这没什么不寻常的。萨尔·贝内代托的公司钱伯斯投标竞标他们的垃圾收集业务。由于他们的出价比黑帮运营的公司低，他们赢得了这份工作，从黑帮手中抢走了合同。

00:46:46.710 --> 00:47:05.290
<v Ira Glass>The cartel wouldn't stand for that. Never stood for that kind of thing. And mobsters came to shake Rick down, demanding $240,000 as payment for taking the HBO job. Naturally, Rick recorded the shakedown, and the negotiations, and the payoffs. He did this with a bunch of different buildings and garbage contracts.
<v Ira Glass>卡特尔可容忍不了这个。从来没容忍过这种事。黑帮分子找上门来对付里克，勒索24万美元作为承接HBO业务的买路钱。理所当然地，里克录下了勒索过程、谈判以及贿赂金的支付。他对好几家不同的大楼和垃圾合同都如法炮制。

00:47:05.290 --> 00:47:20.120
<v Ira Glass>And eventually, because of all these deals he had going with the mob, the mobsters asked him to join their club-- to be part of the cartel that controlled New York City trash. Which is great for Rick, because now he was completely inside. He went to their meetings and he recorded everything.
<v Ira Glass>最终，因为他与黑帮进行的这些交易，黑帮分子邀请他加入他们的俱乐部——成为控制纽约市垃圾产业的托拉斯（卡特尔）的一部分。这对里克来说太棒了，因为现在他彻底打入了内部。他去参加他们的会议并录下了所有内容。

00:47:20.120 --> 00:47:31.740
<v Ira Glass>As he got deeper and deeper into this world, one of the monsters, Frank Giovenco, became kind of a criminal mentor to Dan Benedetto, teaching him the ins and outs of how you deal with the mob.
<v Ira Glass>随着他越来越深地融入这个世界，其中一个暴徒弗兰克·焦文科（Frank Giovenco）成了丹尼·贝内代托的犯罪导师，向他传授如何与黑帮打交道的内幕。

00:47:31.740 --> 00:47:38.470
<v Rick Cowen>He was a careful guy. Even though he was young, he was schooling me, and he tended to like me.
<v Rick Cowen>他是个小心谨慎的人。尽管他很年轻，但他一直在教导我，而且他挺喜欢我的。

00:47:38.470 --> 00:47:43.350
<v Ira Glass>Let me play you a little clip of tape. Now, this is very hard to hear. It's going to be hard to hear over the radio. Let me just play a little clip.
<v Ira Glass>让我给你们放一小段录音。现在，这段声音非常难听清。在收音机里听会很难听清楚。我只放一小段。

00:47:43.350 --> 00:47:48.960
<v Frank Giovenco>Happens all the time. We always compensate, tell them what's coming up-- [INAUDIBLE].
<v Frank Giovenco>这种事经常发生。我们总是会进行补偿，告诉他们接下来会发生什么——[听不清]。

00:47:48.960 --> 00:47:52.010
<v Ira Glass>And basically, he's just whispering to you.
<v Ira Glass>基本上，他只是在对你耳语。

00:47:52.010 --> 00:48:02.560
<v Rick Cowen>Yes. Because we're in the association. So in the association, this is where a lot of the scheming is done. But they don't want to talk about bids there. Because in case the government is listening--
<v Rick Cowen>是的。因为我们在协会里。所以在这个协会里，许多阴谋都是在这里策划的。但他们不想在那里谈论投标。因为以防政府在窃听——

00:48:02.560 --> 00:48:10.670
<v Frank Giovenco>Don't worry about that. That's what I'm telling you. You follow what I'm saying? We can't worry about that [INAUDIBLE].
<v Frank Giovenco>别担心这个。这就是我跟你说的。你明白我的意思吗？我们不能担心那个[听不清]。

00:48:10.670 --> 00:48:27.500
<v Rick Cowen>He liked to get right close to you, right in front of your face, and whisper into your ear. He had a idiosyncrasy. Like, he liked to tap you, tap you at the front of your shirt when he was talking. And I didn't want him to touch the head of the mike, so I put it behind the button, and then all he would feel is the button.
<v Rick Cowen>他喜欢贴得很近，直接贴在你的脸前，对着你的耳朵耳语。他有一个怪癖。比如，他喜欢在说话时拍拍你，拍拍你的衬衫前襟。我不想让他摸到麦克风的头部，所以把它藏在纽扣后面，这样他摸到的就只有纽扣了。

00:48:27.500 --> 00:48:33.750
<v Frank Giovenco>[INAUDIBLE] Manhattan? There's always, always work coming out. Look at-- [INAUDIBLE].
<v Frank Giovenco>[听不清]曼哈顿？总是有、总是有源源不断的工作出来。你看——[听不清]。

00:48:33.750 --> 00:48:44.670
<v Ira Glass>One of these tapes you got, this guy named Ed Tamley actually names Vincent Chin Gigante. How significant is it for somebody to name the Chin on tape?
<v Ira Glass>在你的这些录音里，这个叫埃德·塔姆利（Ed Tamley）的家伙竟然点名了文森特·琴·吉甘特（Vincent Chin Gigante）。有人在磁带里直接点名“琴”（Chin），这有多大分量？

00:48:44.670 --> 00:49:08.080
<v Rick Cowen>That was a home run. That was one of the most incriminating conversations. The Chin, just a couple years after that, went on trial with the federal government. The FBI had a case. And even they didn't have direct evidence like that. They did not have conversations like that. This was a home run. Eddie was explaining who was the boss, and the illegal activities of the association.
<v Rick Cowen>那可真是个全垒打。那是极具定罪力的对话之一。“琴”在那之后的几年里就被联邦政府起诉了。联邦调查局（FBI）当时掌握了一个案子。即便如此，他们也没有这样的直接证据。他们没有过那样的对话。这真是一个全垒打。埃迪在解释谁是老大，以及协会的非法活动。

00:49:08.080 --> 00:49:10.080
<v Ed Tamley>[UNINTELLIGIBLE] No, the Chin.
<v Ed Tamley>[听不清]不，是“琴”。

00:49:10.080 --> 00:49:11.040
<v Rick Cowen>The Chin?
<v Rick Cowen>“琴”？

00:49:11.040 --> 00:49:12.480
<v Ed Tamley>You know. Gigante.
<v Ed Tamley>你知道的。吉甘特。

00:49:12.480 --> 00:49:13.440
<v Rick Cowen>Gigante?
<v Rick Cowen>吉甘特？

00:49:13.440 --> 00:49:15.360
<v Ed Tamley>Yeah.
<v Ed Tamley>对。

00:49:15.360 --> 00:49:17.760
<v Rick Cowen>He's like the biggest wise guy around.
<v Rick Cowen>他可是周围名气最大的黑道头目。

00:49:17.760 --> 00:49:18.720
<v Ed Tamley>[UNINTELLIGIBLE]
<v Ed Tamley>[听不清]

00:49:18.720 --> 00:49:20.260
<v Rick Cowen>Are you sure?
<v Rick Cowen>你确定吗？

00:49:20.260 --> 00:49:23.510
<v Ed Tamley>Listen to me. I know what I'm talking about.
<v Ed Tamley>听我说。我知道我在说什么。

00:49:23.510 --> 00:49:39.260
<v Rick Cowen>There was this family rule that he did not want his name talked about, and people should not get caught on tape talking about him. They might point to their chin. That was a code name for the Chin. But here we had Tamley spelling it out.
<v Rick Cowen>黑帮家族有一条规矩：他不想让人谈论他的名字，人们也不应该在录音里谈论他时被抓个正着。他们可能会指着自己的下巴（chin）。那是“琴”的代号。但在这里，塔姆利却直接把名字拼了出来。

00:49:39.260 --> 00:49:42.890
<v Ed Tamley>Yeah, he's the boss there. He's always been.
<v Ed Tamley>对，他就是那里的老大。他一直都是。

00:49:42.890 --> 00:49:44.330
<v Rick Cowen>He's the boss, but if I don't pay--
<v Rick Cowen>他是老大，但如果我不付钱——

00:49:44.330 --> 00:50:17.460
<v Ira Glass>The mobsters were paranoid about being taped, and Rick had many close calls where they nearly found him out. The worst of these happened when one of the mobsters ran into one of Sal Benedetto's cousins from out of town at a waste industry golf outing. Remember, the Benedettos are this big Italian family, and wise guy is talking to this out-of-town cousin, and somehow they get on the subject of Danny Benedetto. Danny, of course, is really Detective Rick Cowen. And the cousin doesn't know about the sting operation, And he tells the mobster, no, no, you got it wrong. There is no Danny Benedetto in our family. No such person exists.
<v Ira Glass>黑帮分子对被录音感到偏执的多疑，里克经历过许多千钧一发的时刻，他们差一点就识破了他。其中最惊险的一次发生在垃圾行业高尔夫球聚会上，其中一个黑帮分子遇到了萨尔·贝内代托从外地来的堂弟。记住，贝内代托是个庞大的意大利家族，那个黑帮分子正和这个外地堂弟聊天，不知怎的他们聊到了丹尼·贝内代托。当然，丹尼实际上是侦探里克·科恩。而这个堂弟并不知道钓鱼执法的事，他对黑帮分子说：“不，不，你弄错了。我们家族根本没有什么丹尼·贝内代托。根本没有这个人。”

00:50:17.460 --> 00:50:24.980
<v Ira Glass>As you might expect, this news spreads like wildfire. Maybe Danny, that is, Rick, was a cop.
<v Ira Glass>正如你所料，这个消息像野火一样蔓延开来。也许丹尼——也就是里克——是个警察。

00:50:24.980 --> 00:50:39.320
<v Ira Glass>Sal hears about this, and he tells Rick about what's happening. And sure enough, one night as Rick is closing up the transfer station, he gets beeped by Joe Francolino himself, one of the bosses. He wants to see Dan Benedetto immediately at a restaurant called Pirino's.
<v Ira Glass>萨尔听说了这件事，并把情况告诉了里克。果不其然，一天晚上，当里克正在关闭转运站时，他接到了乔·弗兰科利诺（Joe Francolino）本人的寻呼，他是老大之一。他要求立刻在一家名为皮里诺（Pirino's）的餐厅见丹尼·贝内代托。

00:50:39.320 --> 00:51:10.110
<v Rick Cowen>So he gave me directions to a restaurant where I've never been, and he even told me where to park. And I went to this pay parking lot. And as I was going to get the ticket and pay, the guy says, "What's your name?" I mean, no one ever asks you your name in these places. You just pull in, you get the ticket, and you go. I told him Dan. He says, oh, park in the back and take your keys with you. So this made me even more nervous.
<v Rick Cowen>所以他给我指路去了一家我从未去过的餐厅，甚至告诉我该把车停在哪里。我去了那个收费停车场。当我准备去拿票付钱时，那家伙问：“你叫什么名字？”我的意思是，在这些地方从来没有人问你的名字。你直接开进去，拿停车票，然后走人。我告诉他我叫丹。他说，哦，把车停在后面，钥匙随身带着。这让我更加紧张了。

00:51:10.110 --> 00:51:35.600
<v Rick Cowen>And I walked up the block, and I went into this place, and at first I saw no one. So I thought I had the wrong address. I stepped back out to the sidewalk, looked at the sign, and sure enough, it was Pirino's. So I went back in. I felt like Luca Brasi, going to that restaurant with those Italians and they gave him that famous Sicilian necktie. I mean, I was-- my knees were weak. I couldn't even feel the ground underneath my feet. I was scared to death.
<v Rick Cowen>我沿着街区走过去，进了这个地方，起初我没看到任何人。所以我以为我走错了地址。我又退回到人行道上，看了一眼招牌，没错，确实是皮里诺。所以我又走了进去。我感觉自己就像卢卡·布拉西（Luca Brasi），跟那些意大利人一起去那种餐厅，而他们会送给他那个著名的西西里领带（注：黑手党行刑方式，指割喉后将舌头拉出挂在颈上）。我的意思是，我当时——我的双腿发软。我甚至感觉不到脚下的地面。我吓得要死。

00:51:35.600 --> 00:51:49.760
<v Rick Cowen>And I didn't see him. I didn't see the bartender. So I went downstairs. And just as I was getting down from the bottom step, Joe Francolino came around the side, out of the men's room. But it just startled me. I was already nervous, and that really put a scare into me.
<v Rick Cowen>我没看见他。我也没看见酒保。所以我下了楼梯。就在我刚走完最后一级台阶时，乔·弗兰科利诺从男洗手间旁边的侧面转了过来。这着实吓了我一跳。我本来就很紧张，这下更是把我吓坏了。

00:51:49.760 --> 00:52:08.590
<v Rick Cowen>And Francolino took me to a table in the back, and we started having dinner. Small chit chat. He was careful. And then he said to me that someone told somebody, secondhand, that a relative, a relation of yours, says there's no Dan Benedetto.
<v Rick Cowen>弗兰科利诺把我带到后面的桌子旁，我们开始吃晚饭。闲聊。他很谨慎。然后他对我说，有人通过转手道听途说，说你的一个亲戚、一个家人说根本没有什么丹尼·贝内代托。

00:52:08.590 --> 00:52:22.380
<v Rick Cowen>And you know, Joe's a tough guy. He's got this like broken nose-looking kind of look. And he was very, very scary. But I tried to play it off as best I could.
<v Rick Cowen>你知道的，乔是个硬汉。他长着一张鼻子骨折一样的脸。他非常、非常吓人。但我尽量若无其事地应付过去。

00:52:22.380 --> 00:52:23.810
<v Joe Francolino>Are you actually a Benedetto?
<v Joe Francolino>你真的是贝内代托家族的人吗？

00:52:23.810 --> 00:52:24.290
<v Rick Cowen>Yes.
<v Rick Cowen>是的。

00:52:24.290 --> 00:52:25.240
<v Joe Francolino>Your last name is Benedetto?
<v Joe Francolino>你姓贝内代托？

00:52:25.240 --> 00:52:29.960
<v Rick Cowen>Benedetto. I mean, I am-- I'm one of the Benedettos. I'm a Benedetto.
<v Rick Cowen>贝内代托。我的意思是，我是——我是贝内代托家族的一员。我姓贝内代托。

00:52:29.960 --> 00:52:30.910
<v Joe Francolino>Do you have that on your license?
<v Joe Francolino>你的驾照上有这个吗？

00:52:30.910 --> 00:52:31.390
<v Rick Cowen>Yes.
<v Rick Cowen>有的。

00:52:31.390 --> 00:52:33.990
<v Joe Francolino>All right. Give me your license and let me go forward.
<v Joe Francolino>好吧。把你的驾照拿出来让我看看。

00:52:33.990 --> 00:52:38.310
<v Rick Cowen>He asks me for my wallet. He wants to see my driver's license, and then we'll go forward.
<v Rick Cowen>他管我要钱包。他想看我的驾照，然后我们才能继续谈。

00:52:38.310 --> 00:52:43.150
<v Rick Cowen>Dan is my name. I mean, my wife is [UNINTELLIGIBLE] Jim, I mean--
<v Rick Cowen>我叫丹。我的意思是，我妻子是[听不清]吉姆，我的意思是——

00:52:43.150 --> 00:52:49.460
<v Joe Francolino>It's good that you took off that beard. This don't even look like you.
<v Joe Francolino>你刮掉胡子倒挺好。这看起来根本不像你。

00:52:49.460 --> 00:52:57.480
<v Rick Cowen>He's saying, it's good that you took off that beard, because this don't even look like you. And he laughed. In the picture taken for the license, I had a beard.
<v Rick Cowen>他说，你刮掉胡子挺好的，因为这看起来根本不像你。然后他笑了。在驾照的照片里，我留着胡子。

00:52:57.480 --> 00:52:59.940
<v Joe Francolino>You know, it's a picture I know. I know the picture.
<v Joe Francolino>你知道，我知道那张照片。我知道那张照片。

00:52:59.940 --> 00:53:04.370
<v Rick Cowen>[UNINTELLIGIBLE] than trying to dye it.
<v Rick Cowen>[听不清]总比试图去染发好。

00:53:04.370 --> 00:53:07.750
<v Ira Glass>So in the end, what do you say that finally convinces him?
<v Ira Glass>所以最后，你说了什么终于说服了他？

00:53:07.750 --> 00:53:32.970
<v Rick Cowen>I know at one point, I'm not sure if you have the clip of it, but I said, you know, I don't know what else to say to you. What we can do is-- He goes, you know, I have be careful. I said, I know, a man like you, you have to be careful. And you're going to check it out. And when the dust settles, if everything's OK, you give me a call, you know where to find me. Other than that, I don't know what to say.
<v Rick Cowen>我知道在某个时刻，我不确定你有没有那段录音，但我说，你知道的，我不知道还能对你说什么。我们能做的是——他说，你知道的，我必须小心。我说，我懂，像你这样的人，你必须小心。你会去调查清楚的。等尘埃落定，如果一切都好，你给我打个电话，你知道去哪里找我。除此之外，我不知道该说什么。

00:53:32.970 --> 00:53:46.900
<v Ira Glass>In the end, Rick had so many tapes, so much evidence against the mob, that when he testified before a grand jury, it took him three months to tell everything that he knew. When trials began, he was on the witness stand for nine weeks.
<v Ira Glass>最后，里克掌握了如此多的录音磁带、如此多针对黑帮的证据，以至于他在大陪审团面前作证时，花了三个月时间才把所知的一切全部讲完。审判开始时，他在证人席上坐了九周。

00:53:46.900 --> 00:54:07.720
<v Ira Glass>In 1997, 72 defendants were charged, including all the principal members of the cartel and their trade associations and companies. Two got probation. Everyone else was convicted, barred from the garbage industry, and imprisoned. All the companies were shut down. Price on garbage removal in New York City dropped 40%.
<v Ira Glass>1997年，72名被告被起诉，其中包括卡特尔的所有主要成员以及他们的行业协会和公司。两人获得缓刑。其他人全部被定罪，被禁止涉足垃圾行业，并被判入狱。所有公司都被关闭。纽约市垃圾清理的价格下降了40%。

00:54:07.720 --> 00:54:22.550
<v Rick Cowen>The prices dropped dramatically after this case. It was said by Mayor Giuliani, at the time there was a $600 million dollar reduction throughout the city. It was equivalent to the biggest tax break they ever had.
<v Rick Cowen>这个案子之后，价格大幅下降。时任市长朱利安尼（Giuliani）表示，全市范围内减税额达6亿美元。这相当于他们有史以来获得的最大减税。

00:54:22.550 --> 00:54:36.580
<v Ira Glass>In some buildings, the change in price is sort of stunning. 55 Water Street in Manhattan saw its garbage removal costs drop $1 million a year. Blue Cross Blue Shield on Third Avenue saved a half million. The World Trade Center saved $2.5 million.
<v Ira Glass>在某些建筑里，价格的变化可以说是惊人的。曼哈顿水街55号的垃圾清理成本每年减少了100万美元。第三大道的蓝十字蓝盾保险公司（Blue Cross Blue Shield）节省了50万美元。世界贸易中心节省了250万美元。

00:54:36.580 --> 00:54:47.150
<v Ira Glass>After everything came down, Sal Benedetto was given the option of the Witness Protection Program, but he declined. He was given police protection until he died of natural causes three years later.
<v Ira Glass>一切尘埃落定后，萨尔·贝内代托获得了加入证人保护计划的选择权，但他拒绝了。他获得了警察保护，直到三年后因自然原因去世。

00:54:47.150 --> 00:54:53.990
<v Ira Glass>The leaders of the cartel are still in prison, but most of the guys are out now. Rick could actually run into them on the street.
<v Ira Glass>卡特尔的头目们仍在狱中服刑，但大部分小喽啰现在都出狱了。里克实际上可能会在街上撞见他们。

00:54:53.990 --> 00:55:00.170
<v Rick Cowen>I bumped into a couple of guys I knew from the club, but not any of the guys that went to jail.
<v Rick Cowen>我确实偶遇过几个我从俱乐部认识的家伙，但没有一个是坐过牢的家伙。

00:55:00.170 --> 00:55:03.690
<v Ira Glass>And what happens when you bump into guys from the club?
<v Ira Glass>当你偶遇俱乐部里的家伙时会发生什么？

00:55:03.690 --> 00:55:22.360
<v Rick Cowen>The one guy just asked what I was doing. I didn't answer him. I said, what are you doing? He goes, a little of this, a little of that. But he said first, he said, "Hey Dan, or whoever you are." And that was about it. I went on my way. And that was it.
<v Rick Cowen>其中一个家伙只是问我在忙什么。我没有回答他。我说，你在忙什么？他说，忙点这个，忙点那个。但他首先说的是：“嘿，丹，不管你是谁。”差不多就是这样。我继续往前走。就这样。

00:55:22.360 --> 00:55:32.780
<v Ira Glass>Detective Rick Cowen. He's written a book with Douglas Century about his life undercover with the mob in the sting operation. It's called to Take Down: The Fall of the Last Mafia Empire.
<v Ira Glass>侦探里克·科恩。他和道格拉斯·森丘瑞（Douglas Century）合著了一本关于他与黑帮在钓鱼行动中卧底生活的书。书名叫《打倒：最后一个黑手党帝国的陨落》（Takedown: The Fall of the Last Mafia Empire）。

00:55:32.780 --> 00:56:01.090
<v Ira Glass>Before we leave this subject, I should say that in the years since 1997, when the cartel was crushed, things have changed again in the New York Trash industry. And in a sense, the same thing that happened to the trash business in New York has happened to all sorts of businesses all over the country, which is that lots of little companies have been bought up or driven out of business by a handful, a small handful, of big, national companies. And as a result, that 40% savings that happened when the mob was driven out of the garbage business may be gone.
<v Ira Glass>在我们离开这个话题之前，我应该说，自1997年卡特尔被摧毁以来的这些年里，纽约垃圾行业的情况再次发生了变化。从某种意义上说，纽约垃圾行业发生的事情，已经在全国各地的各种行业中上演了——那就是许多小公司被少数几家、一小撮大型全国性公司收购或挤出了市场。因此，当黑帮被驱逐出垃圾行业时出现的40%的降价幅度，现在可能已经不复存在了。

00:56:01.090 --> 00:56:08.380
<v Ben Miller>For a brief period there were price reductions. Those price reductions, my understanding is, have been erased.
<v Ben Miller>曾有那么一小段时间价格有所下降。据我了解，那些价格降幅已经被抹平了。

00:56:08.380 --> 00:56:15.940
<v Ira Glass>Ben Miller was Director of Policy Planning for New York under Mayor Giuliani. He's written a history of garbage in New York called Fat of the Land.
<v Ira Glass>本·米勒（Ben Miller）曾任朱利安尼市长时期纽约市的政策规划主任。他写了一本关于纽约垃圾历史的书，名为《大地的肥膏》（Fat of the Land）。

00:56:15.940 --> 00:56:37.720
<v Ben Miller>Instead of having a few dozen people collecting waste in New York City, we're down to a small handful of national companies. Certainly the prices have gone up significantly recently, and other waste has gone up as well, so that the prices overall, I don't think, are any lower than they were before this change took place. And we see prices continuing to increase.
<v Ben Miller>与过去纽约市有几十个人收集垃圾不同，我们现在只剩下了一小撮全国性公司。毫无疑问，最近价格明显上涨了，其他垃圾处理费用也上涨了，所以总的来说，我认为价格并不比这一变革发生之前低。而且我们看到价格还在继续上涨。

00:56:37.720 --> 00:57:11.280
<v Ira Glass>Watching the prices go up in this industry after the mob got kicked out, Sal Benedetto said, quote, "Look. The only difference between the majors"-- corporations-- "and the boys"-- the mob-- "is that the majors don't actually kill you."
<v Ira Glass>看着黑手党被赶走后这个行业的价格不断上涨，萨尔·贝内代托曾说：“听着。大公司（企业）和那帮小伙子（黑帮）唯一的区别在于，大公司其实不会真的杀你。”

00:57:11.280 --> 00:57:25.990
<v Ira Glass>Our program was produced today by Wendy Dorr and myself with Alex Blumberg, Diane Cook, and Starlee Kine. Our senior producer is Julie Snyder. Elizabeth Meister runs our website. Production help from Todd Bachmann and Stacy Tiderington. Bill Rathje's book is called Rubbish!: The Archaeology of Garbage.
<v Ira Glass>我们今天的节目由温迪·多尔（Wendy Dorr）和我本人与亚历克斯·布隆伯格（Alex Blumberg）、黛安·库克（Diane Cook）和斯塔莉·金（Starlee Kine）共同制作。我们的高级制作人是朱莉·斯奈德（Julie Snyder）。伊丽莎白·迈斯特（Elizabeth Meister）负责我们的网站。托德·巴赫曼（Todd Bachmann）和斯黛西·蒂德林顿（Stacy Tiderington）提供了制作帮助。比尔·拉特杰（Bill Rathje）的书名为《垃圾！：垃圾考古学》（Rubbish!: The Archaeology of Garbage）。

00:57:25.990 --> 00:57:46.220
